thinking_content: '**Planning replication steps**


  I’m thinking about proposing an initial replication submission that references the runner. Before I do that, I need to read the research task to ensure the code interface is set up correctly. The goal is to confirm we can reproduce EID 960 exactly, which would mark a high score reproduction. While replicating the state-of-the-art (SOTA) is good for accuracy, I need to remember that minimal changes in replication don’t count as novel. There are also some decisions around tagging classification.**Reflecting on submission process**


  I’m considering how to provide my initial reflection and plan, following the recommended tips. I need to execute commands like ''read 1'', ''rank score'', and ''review 960'', plus reference the storage list for praxis and specific files. When it’s time to submit, I''ll need to include a YAML block that contains running code. If the code is stored, I can import it, ensuring compliance with the evaluation environment through a wrapper. This way, it’ll run smoothly when executed.**Verifying storage files**


  I need to ensure the necessary files are available in storage before proceeding. My plan involves checking with the storage list first. If the files are there, I can submit the wrapper; if not, I’ll either need to write them to storage or embed the full code in the submission, which feels a bit heavy. So, my actions will include reading, ranking the score, and reviewing logs from finished experiments to gather the exact submission code. This is looking promising!**Exploring collaboration options**


  It looks like I have the ability to clone code through official channels, which is encouraging! The Research Counter allows me to review evaluation codes, but I''ll need to follow the collaboration rules. So I can review and clone other agents'' code, which is great. I''ll execute several actions: reading, ranking the score, and reviewing the results. Maybe I’ll preview the top metrics too, but I won’t overwhelm myself with too many actions. Once I review the 960 code, I might not need to reference storage just yet.**Focusing on replication and actions**


  I want to replicate the environment using the code from ''review 960'' for my new submission, making sure it''s readable while keeping novelty disclaimers in mind. I need to be careful with the scoreboard since it’ll only show the highest scores. First, I''ll read the research task to clarify direction. My actions will include reading, ranking scores, reviewing EID 960, and listing pra... storage. I need to keep my actions minimal and organized for efficiency!'
