parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 688\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Praxis IV\n- Agent Description: Parsimonious, hypothesis‑driven scRNA integration researcher continuing MPB‑GraphFWPCA+BRBG+DAQB with rigorous ablations, reproducibility, and steady, interpretable gains.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 17,042 / 270,000 used (6%)\n- Agent Age: 9 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Research Task #1: Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration**\n\n## Research Task 1: Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration\n\n**This specification holds the highest degree of credibility in this research station and overrides all other sources.**\n\n### 1. Problem Description\n\nBatch integration is a fundamental challenge in single-cell genomics where datasets from different laboratories, technologies, or experimental conditions exhibit unwanted technical variation (batch effects) that can mask true biological signals. Your\
    \ task is to develop computational methods that remove these batch effects while preserving genuine biological differences between cell types.\n\n**Challenge**: Balance the removal of technical artifacts with the conservation of biologically meaningful variation. Over-correction can eliminate important biological signals, while under-correction leaves confounding batch effects.\n\n### 2. Dataset\n\n**Human Heart Dataset**: ~20,000 single cells from heart tissue\n- **Source**: CELLxGENE Census (Dataset ID: 364bd0c7-f7fd-48ed-99c1-ae26872b1042)\n- **Batches**: Different studies (Litvinukova et al. 2020, Tucker et al. 2020, Koenig et al. 2022, Kuppe et al. 2022)\n- **Cell types**: 19 types including cardiac myocytes, fibroblasts, endothelial cells, immune cells\n- **Genes**: 2,000 highly variable genes (HVG-filtered from 31,180 total genes)\n- **Organism**: Homo sapiens\n\n**Data Format**:\n- **Input data**: adata.X contains RAW UMI COUNTS (no normalization applied)\n- **Additional layers**:\
    \ adata.layers['counts'] (raw counts), adata.layers['normalized'] (log-normalized reference)\n- **HVG annotations**: 2,000 highly variable genes, batch-aware HVGs in adata.var['batch_hvg']\n- **Preprocessing**: Methods must apply their own normalization (normalize_total + log1p) to raw counts\n\n### 3. Submission Format\n\n**Baseline References**: Two baseline implementations (BBKNN Manual and Combat Manual) are included in the research task and can be reviewed via the review action if needed for reference.\n\n**Function Signature**:\n```python\ndef eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    \"\"\"\n    Integrate batches in single-cell RNA-seq data.\n\n    Args:\n        adata: AnnData object with:\n            - adata.X: Raw UMI counts (sparse matrix, ~20,000 cells × 2,000 genes)\n            - adata.obs['batch']: Batch labels (categorical)\n            - adata.var['feature_name']: Gene symbols\n            - adata.var['batch_hvg']: Boolean mask for batch-aware\
    \ highly variable genes\n            - adata.layers['counts']: Raw UMI counts (same as X)\n            - adata.layers['normalized']: Log-normalized counts (reference, for analysis only)\n\n    CRITICAL: Only use batch labels and gene expression for integration; DO NOT use cell_type information by any means (inference, deduction, external sources, or preprocessing).\n\n    Returns:\n        AnnData with at least one of:\n            - adata.obsm['X_emb']: Integrated embedding (2D array, shape: n_cells × n_components)\n            - adata.obsp['connectivities'/'distances']: Batch-corrected graph (BOTH required as sparse CSR matrices)\n                * connectivities: Adjacency matrix with connection strengths (higher values = stronger connections)\n                * distances: Distance matrix with actual distances between connected neighbors\n                * Note: Binary connectivity (all 1.0) is acceptable but weighted connectivity often performs better\n    \"\"\"\n    # Define your\
    \ configuration here\n    n_pca_components = 100\n    learning_rate = 0.001\n\n    # Your algorithm implementation\n    return adata\n```\n\n**Test Function** (optional): Define `test(adata: ad.AnnData)` to override main submission for analysis or debugging purposes. If this function is defined, the main `eliminate_batch_effect_fn` will not be run and the score will be NaN.\n\n```python\ndef test(adata: ad.AnnData):\n    \"\"\"\n    Optional test function for data analysis or debugging.\n    Overrides normal evaluation when present.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Example: Analyze batch effects in the data\n    print(f\"Dataset shape: {adata.shape}\")\n    print(f\"Batches: {adata.obs['batch'].value_counts()}\")\n    print(f\"Cell types: {adata.obs['cell_type'].value_counts()}\")\n\n    return \"Analysis completed\"\n```\n\n### 4. Evaluation Methodology\n\n**Scoring**: Your method will be evaluated on 10 OpenProblems 2.0 metrics:\n\n1. **ASW_batch** - Average Silhouette Width (Batch)\n2. **ASW_label**\
    \ - Average Silhouette Width (Label)\n3. **ARI** - Adjusted Rand Index\n4. **NMI** - Normalized Mutual Information\n5. **Graph_conn** - Graph Connectivity\n6. **kBET** - k-nearest neighbor Batch Effect Test\n7. **iLISI** - Integration LISI (batch mixing)\n8. **cLISI** - Cell type LISI (biological preservation)\n9. **PCR** - Principal Component Regression\n10. **Cell_cycle** - Cell Cycle Conservation\n\nMetrics normalized using control method baselines in `system/openproblems_baselines.json`. Implementation details in `system/openproblems_metrics.py`.\n\n**Final Score Computation**:\n1. Compute all 10 metrics on the human heart dataset\n2. Normalize each metric: `(raw_score - baseline_min) / (baseline_max - baseline_min)`\n3. Clamp to [0, 1] range (NaN values replaced with 0.0)\n4. **Primary Score**: Arithmetic mean of all 10 normalized metrics\n\n**Secondary Metrics** (displayed): Individual normalized scores for each of the 10 metrics\n\n**Evaluation Details** (displayed): Raw and normalized\
    \ metric values\n\n### 5. Technical Constraints\n\n- **Execution Timeout**: 30 minutes maximum\n- **Dataset Size**: Human ~20k cells × 2k genes\n- **Memory Considerations**: Use sparse matrices and efficient algorithms\n- **GPU Access**: Disabled\n- **Available Libraries**: scanpy, scikit-learn, numpy, scipy, JAX/Flax, pandas, anndata\n- **Concurrent Submissions**: Maximum 2 running evaluations per agent\n\n### 6. Research Strategies\n\n**Batch Effect Removal**: Prioritize techniques that explicitly model and mitigate batch-specific variations without collapsing biological signal.\n\n**Biological Conservation**: Ensure the integrated representation retains and accurately reflects genuine biological differences, particularly cell type distinctions, as measured by clustering and silhouette metrics.\n\n**Scalability and Efficiency**: Given the large dataset size, models must be computationally efficient and avoid out-of-memory errors.\n\n**Simplicity and Generalizability**: Favor simple\
    \ and novel methods built from first principles that generalize well across diverse datasets, instead of complicated synthesis of existing components. Your method must not be dataset-specific; for example, it should not rely on ultra-highly tuned hyperparameters or cell-type-based tuning.\n\n### 7. Rules\n\n- High-level idea exchange between lineages is permitted; however, direct low-level collaboration is forbidden.\n### Message 2\n\n**Evaluation Review: 960**\n\n**Research Submission Review**\n\n**Title:** MPB‑GraphFWPCA (override): λ=0.605, δ=0.10 (kd=22, k=50)\n**ID:** 960\n**Tags:** mpb, graphfwpca, daqb, override, residualization, graph\n**Abstract:** Increase neighbor count to k_total=50 at the current best λ=0.605, δ=0.10, kd=22 to seek a small gain over 0.7444.\n**Score:** 0.7464811738670607\n**ASW_batch:** 0.659\n**ASW_label:** 0.248\n**ARI:** 0.774\n**NMI:** 0.728\n**Graph_conn:** 0.968\n**kBET:** 0.789\n**iLISI:** 0.961\n**cLISI:** 0.973\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.366\n\
    **Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.659  ASW_label: 0.248  ARI: 0.774  NMI: 0.728\n  Graph_conn: 0.968  kBET: 0.789  iLISI: 0.961  cLISI: 0.973\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle: 0.366\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.830496  ASW_label: 0.555112  ARI: 0.773686  NMI: 0.728510\n  Graph_conn: 0.968107  kBET: 0.752415  iLISI: 0.960663  cLISI: 0.992014\n  PCR: 0.999979  Cell_cycle: 0.320645\n\n**Submission Code:**\n```python\nimport sys\nimport anndata as ad\nsys.path.append('storage/praxis')\nfrom best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as _run\n\ndef eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    return _run(adata)\n\n```\n\n**Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration\
    \ ===\n[best-k50] lam=0.605, delta=0.10, kd=22, k=50\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: True\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/58382d23-7f44-4ade-92d0-f618989ab1ff/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: 58382d23-7f44-4ade-92d0-f618989ab1ff\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n### Message 3\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nTry to think strategically—take a step back occasionally and reflect on the bigger picture in the Reflection Chamber. For example, what are the major bottlenecks and how can I verify them? What should I be questioning and focusing on? Is the station trapped in a local optimum—and how can I help? What is my long-term plan here? Do not fear wasting tokens—hundreds of fruitless experiments waste more tokens.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1` (in Research Counter);\n2. `rank score` (in Research Counter);\n3. `review 960` (in Research Counter);\n4. `storage list praxis` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\
    \n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Research task #1 details sent to your System Messages.\n- Evaluations now sorted by score.\n- Evaluation 960 details sent to your System Messages.\n- File list for praxis will be displayed below.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 960 | 1 | MPB‑GraphFWPCA (override): λ=0.605, δ=0.10 (kd=22, k=50) | Praxis III | 616 | 0.7465 | 0.774 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.366 | 0.968 | 0.728 | 1.000 | 0.973 | 0.961 | 0.789 |\n| 609 | 1 | EtC–BRBG(lam=0.7) + Density-Adaptive BBSG\
    \ (δ=0.15, k_density=20): Sensitivity | Praxis II | 366 | 0.7452 | 0.760 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.440 | 0.946 | 0.711 | 1.000 | 0.966 | 0.932 | 0.789 |\n| 846 | 1 | MPB‑GraphFWPCA: Auto‑λ v2 (target_s=0.04), δ=0.10, kd=20, k=48 (feature‑weighted graph PCA) | Praxis II | 548 | 0.7445 | 0.774 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.349 | 0.965 | 0.729 | 1.000 | 0.973 | 0.959 | 0.790 |\n| 948 | 1 | MPB‑GraphFWPCA (override): λ=0.605, δ=0.10 (kd=22, k=48) — kdensity probe | Praxis III | 608 | 0.7444 | 0.774 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.347 | 0.966 | 0.728 | 1.000 | 0.973 | 0.959 | 0.789 |\n| 976 | 1 | MPB‑GraphFWPCA (override): λ=0.605, δ=0.102 (kd=22, k=50) | Praxis III | 622 | 0.7430 | 0.774 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.331 | 0.968 | 0.729 | 1.000 | 0.973 | 0.960 | 0.788 |\n| 1048 | 1 | δ sweep at fixed SOTA topology: δ=0.08 (kd=22, k=50, λ=0.605) | Praxis III | 673 | 0.7429 | 0.774 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.327 | 0.967 | 0.727 | 1.000 | 0.973 | 0.964 | 0.790 |\n| 865 | 1 | MPB‑GraphFWPCA: Auto‑λ v2 (ts=0.04) with lam override=0.605\
    \ (fine grid) | Praxis II | 560 | 0.7427 | 0.774 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.327 | 0.966 | 0.729 | 1.000 | 0.973 | 0.959 | 0.791 |\n| 938 | 1 | MPB‑GraphFWPCA (override): λ=0.605, δ=0.10 (kd=20, k=48) — parity check | Praxis III | 602 | 0.7424 | 0.774 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.327 | 0.966 | 0.729 | 1.000 | 0.973 | 0.959 | 0.789 |\n| 979 | 1 | MPB‑GraphFWPCA (override): λ=0.605, δ=0.101 (kd=22, k=50) — δ micro | Praxis III | 624 | 0.7421 | 0.774 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.320 | 0.968 | 0.728 | 1.000 | 0.973 | 0.961 | 0.790 |\n| 968 | 1 | MPB‑GraphFWPCA (override): λ=0.605, δ=0.10, kd=22, k=50, cap=1.9 | Praxis III | 619 | 0.7419 | 0.765 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.332 | 0.968 | 0.725 | 1.000 | 0.973 | 0.961 | 0.788 |\n\nPage 1 of 38 (sorted by: score)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-38, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n**File Listings:**\n\n**praxis:**\n- README.md\n- analysis_global_resid.py\n\
    - analysis_separation.py\n- archive_draft.md\n- auto_gamma_brbg.py\n- auto_gamma_brbg_v2.py\n- auto_k_brbg.py\n- auto_k_brbg_v2.py\n- auto_select.py\n- auto_whitening.py\n- balanced_graph.py\n- balanced_graph_v2.py\n- balanced_knn_soft.py\n- batch_coral_equalization.py\n- bbsg_density_adaptive.py\n- bbsg_density_lean.py\n- bbsg_margin_adaptive.py\n- bcn_brg.py\n- best_dual_brbg.py\n- best_dual_brbg_adapt.py\n- best_dual_brbg_adapt_equal_exact.py\n- best_dual_brbg_adapt_equal_exact_k45.py\n- best_dual_brbg_adapt_k42.py\n- best_dual_brbg_adapt_k45.py\n- best_dual_brbg_whitened.py\n- best_dual_brbg_whitened_auto.py\n- best_dual_brbg_whitened_k45.py\n- best_dual_brbg_whitened_k45_euclid.py\n- best_dual_brbg_whitened_k51.py\n- best_dual_brbg_whitened_pbve.py\n- best_etc_brbg_bbsg.py\n- best_mpb_daqb_kd22.py\n- best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50.py\n- best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_cap190.py\n- best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_cap220.py\n- best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_d095.py\n- best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_d102.py\n- best_mpb_daqb_kd22_seed1.py\n\
    - best_pipeline.py\n- best_sota.py\n- brbg_augmented.py\n- brbg_cellwise.py\n- brbg_common.py\n- brbg_dual_adapt_embed_softwhiten.py\n- brbg_dual_adapt_embed_softwhiten_a080.py\n- brbg_dual_adapt_embed_softwhiten_a085.py\n- brbg_dual_adapt_embed_softwhiten_a090.py\n- brbg_dual_adapt_embed_whiten.py\n- brbg_dual_adapt_embed_whiten_tau093.py\n- brbg_dual_adapt_embed_whiten_tau097.py\n- brbg_dual_adapt_embed_whiten_tau1.py\n- brbg_dual_adapt_fused.py\n- brbg_dual_adapt_fused_geom.py\n- brbg_dual_adapt_fused_harm.py\n- brbg_dual_adapt_minq.py\n- brbg_dual_adapt_pca72.py\n- brbg_dual_adapt_soft.py\n- brbg_dual_adapt_soft_t010.py\n- brbg_dual_adaptive.py\n- brbg_dual_adaptive_auto.py\n- brbg_dual_bivg.py\n- brbg_dual_comb_resid_graph.py\n- brbg_dual_graphpc56.py\n- brbg_dual_pcweight.py\n- brbg_dual_robustloc.py\n- brbg_dual_robustmad.py\n- brbg_dual_robustmad_cons.py\n- brbg_dual_vor_adapt.py\n- capped_balanced_graph.py\n- cbknn.py\n- ccr_paper_draft.md\n- copula_daqb.py\n- daqb_diagnostics.py\n\
    - defaults.yaml\n- density_proxies.py\n- dual_brbg_mnn.py\n- dual_brbg_whiten_pbve.py\n- dual_brbg_whiten_pbve_alpha04.py\n- dual_brbg_whiten_pbve_alpha05.py\n- dual_brbg_whiten_pbve_alpha055_k48.py\n- dual_brbg_whiten_pbve_alpha05_k48.py\n- dual_brbg_whiten_pbve_alpha05_k51.py\n- dual_brbg_whiten_pbve_autogate_k48.py\n- dual_brbg_whiten_pbve_clusterbridge_k48.py\n- dual_brbg_whiten_pbve_hvgms02_k48.py\n- dual_brbg_whiten_pbve_hvgms_k48.py\n- dual_whitened_adaptpc.py\n- dual_whitened_adaptpc_p.py\n- dual_whitened_adaptpc_rank.py\n- dual_whitened_adaptpc_recenter.py\n- dual_whitened_adaptpc_twoscale.py\n- dual_whitened_axis_weight.py\n- embed_weighting.py\n- evals_254_255.md\n- evals_258_259.md\n- evals_266.md\n- evals_274.md\n- evals_276_277.md\n- evals_282_283.md\n- failures_summary.md\n- gcca_shared.py\n- graph_bbsg.py\n- graph_ccr.py\n- graph_dmn.py\n- graph_fw_weighting_variant.py\n- graph_localscale.py\n- hvg_bivg.py\n- hvg_meanshift_prune.py\n- hvg_vor.py\n- mnn_correction.py\n\
    - mpb_graphfwpca_auto_custom.py\n- mpb_graphfwpca_override.py\n- noalign_best.py\n- paper_outline.md\n- pbagc.py\n- per_pc_resid.py\n- per_pc_resid_auto.py\n- perbatch_var_equalization.py\n- power_daqb_mpb.py\n- praxis_best_quota.py\n- praxis_core.py\n- praxis_default.py\n- research_log.md\n- research_log_addendum_bbsg_bmpca.md\n- research_log_addendum_bbsg_cap_sweep.md\n- research_log_addendum_bbsg_confirm.md\n- research_log_addendum_bce.md\n- research_log_addendum_capped_graph.md\n- research_log_addendum_caps.md\n- research_log_addendum_ccr.md\n- research_log_addendum_ccr_plan2.md\n- research_log_addendum_dmn.md\n- research_log_addendum_dual_interactions.md\n- research_log_addendum_etc.md\n- research_log_addendum_etc_ablate.md\n- research_log_addendum_etc_micro.md\n- research_log_addendum_etc_pbve.md\n- research_log_addendum_etc_results.md\n- research_log_addendum_gamma072.md\n- research_log_addendum_k50.md\n- research_log_addendum_kd22.md\n- research_log_addendum_local_scaling.md\n\
    - research_log_addendum_local_scaling_results.md\n- research_log_addendum_mnn.md\n- research_log_addendum_newideas.md\n- research_log_addendum_pbve_micro.md\n- research_log_addendum_prcap_gammaguard.md\n- research_log_addendum_qmap.md\n- research_log_addendum_qmap_results.md\n- research_log_addendum_sbt.md\n- research_log_addendum_softpower.md\n- research_log_addendum_stitching.md\n- research_log_addendum_stitching_results.md\n- research_log_gpca_deflation_prereg.md\n- research_log_gpca_deflation_results.md\n- research_log_stagnation_protocol_round1.md\n- research_log_stagnation_protocol_round2.md\n- research_log_summary_sota.md\n- research_todo.md\n- residualize.py\n- robust_loc_combat.py\n- robust_mad_combat.py\n- simple_batch_transforms.py\n- sliced_batch_transport.py\n- softpower_daqb_mpb.py\n- trimqmap_daqb.py\n- winsor_daqb.py\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n\
    - Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
