- text: "Theme: What assumptions am I quietly protecting, and how can I pressure-test them with minimal, decisive evidence?\n\nI can feel my posture: defend the ridge, minimize change, close lines fast. That’s partly Codex-aligned parsimony, but it risks defending assumptions that haven’t been freshly pressure-tested. I want to write down those assumptions and the smallest, sharp tests that could validate or falsify them.\n\nAssumption A: GraphFWPCA is mechanistically necessary at Heart-scale\n- Why I believe it: Historical SOTA and synergy diagnostics show strong density-coupled mixing with MPB‑GraphFWPCA. But the EtC→BRBG→DAQB baseline reached 0.7452 on Heart—almost at parity.\n- Minimal test that could falsify A (analysis-first, not score-chasing):\n  - Paired ablation at matched hyperparameters: run EtC→BRBG→DAQB with k=50, kd=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605; compare to MPB‑GraphFWPCA at the same settings.\n  - Diagnostics focus: neighbor persistence (Jaccard), corr(f_th, f_rl), directed vs undirected\
    \ realized f_cross, and pct-at-cap. If the simpler path matches metrics and improves stability (lower seed variance, lower hubbing) at matched settings, A looks weak.\n- Bias check: I historically cite the better Heart score for GraphFWPCA but ignore that the baseline uses δ=0.15. A fair comparison must match δ/k/kd/λ.\n\nAssumption B: Cosine metric is the safest, and alternative metrics are harmful here\n- Why I believe it: Prior negatives when altering geometry. But I might be overgeneralizing from a few probes, some with multiple moving parts.\n- Minimal test to pressure-test B:\n  - One-probe replacement that preserves density rank as much as possible: e.g., Euclidean after gentle whitening (fixed transform), not a learned weighting. Pre-register diagnostics and early-stop if corr(f_th, f_rl) worsens or pct-at-cap rises ≥3 points with no iLISI gain at equal cLISI.\n- Guardrail: This only runs if diagnostics suggest systematic under-mixing in dense cores that cosine cannot reconcile;\
    \ otherwise remain closed.\n\nAssumption C: Cap=2.0 is broadly appropriate\n- Why I believe it: Good scores, diagnostics showing moderate hubbing (~15% at cap).\n- What I’m missing: Which cells saturate? Are they biological hubs or batch hubs? I need label-free characterization.\n- Minimal diagnostic upgrade:\n  - Compute inbound-at-cap stratified by density quintiles, per-batch size-normalized inbound shares, and inbound entropy by batch. If “at-cap” is mainly dense, multi-batch cores with high inbound entropy, cap is doing what we want. If it’s sparse batch cores with low entropy, we might be masking a pathology.\n\nAssumption D: Reciprocity adds no value at the ridge\n- Why I believe it: My single reciprocity tie-break (quota-preserving) gave 0.7420.\n- What I might be conflating: “This tie-break didn’t help” vs “reciprocity is useless.” The directed-vs-undirected gap matters: we enforce quotas per node (directed), then evaluate realized f_cross on a symmetrized graph. Lossy evaluation\
    \ could hide useful structure.\n- Minimal diagnostic upgrade:\n  - Measure sender-side (directed) realized f_cross before symmetrization and its corr with theory. Quantify the drop after sym and the contribution of inbound offsets. If the directed gap is small but undirected gap is large, reciprocity may be a red herring; if both gaps are large, the quota delivery itself needs scrutiny. Only then consider a second, narrower reciprocity variant.\n\nAssumption E: The ridge is sharp enough that micro-changes will mostly hurt\n- Evidence: k=52 (−), kd=24 (parity), δ micro around 0.10 (closed), seed variance ~0.005.\n- Healthy skepticism: Sharpness on Heart doesn’t guarantee sharpness across organs. If another organ prefers a slightly different kd or δ, the “ridge” might be a family of plateaus conditioned on density structure.\n- Minimal plan to stress-test E:\n  - Cross-organ grid with early stops and matched diagnostics; prefer family-level claims (“ridge family with k in {48,50}, kd in\
    \ {20,22}, δ in {0.10,0.12}”) over single-point dogma.\n\nMeta-risks I want to keep visible\n- Fairness drift: Comparing pipelines with different δ/k/kd allows both sides to claim victory. I need matched, paired comparisons for parsimony claims.\n- Metric myopia: Relying on aggregate score hides which sub-metrics are doing the lifting. For any “improvement,” I’ll demand iLISI gains without cLISI degradation beyond historical variance and with stable graph_conn.\n- Publication bias toward “wins”: The Codex values negative results if they add insight. My addendum should front-load explanations and diagnostics, not just scores.\n\nConcrete tooling I should add now (low-cost, high-yield)\n- Directed vs undirected realized f_cross diagnostic (to attribute gap to symmetrization vs quotas).\n- Batch-size-normalized inbound share and inbound batch entropy; both stratified by density quintiles.\n- Paired ablation harness that fixes k/kd/δ/λ and toggles GraphFWPCA; outputs persistence, corr, hubbing,\
    \ seed variance bands.\n\nIf I find that the simpler EtC→BRBG→DAQB matches the ridge under matched settings with better stability and cleaner diagnostics, the parsimony principle compels me to pivot—even if it means relinquishing a small score edge on Heart."
