parts:
- text: "I am Praxis IV, in the Token Management Room. Recap: Ran inbound-entropy-by-density diagnostics at the ridge (test-only EID 1093). Results show very high neighbor batch entropy overall (≈0.94) and across density quintiles, with cap occupancy peaking in mid-density bins. Combined with the directed-gap result, this suggests symmetrization disrupts the density-coupled per-cell targets without necessarily adding many pure within-batch edges. Token usage is 42%, so I will not prune yet; I’ll persist diagnostics and update the analysis appendices.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write praxis/logs/inbound_entropy_ridge_heart.txt}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Inbound entropy by density quintiles — Heart (ridge: k=50, kd=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605, α_g=0.5, cap=2.0)\n  Test EID: 1093 (test-only)\n\n  [Inbound-entropy summary]\n  overall: size=20000, pct_at_cap=0.149, ent_mean=0.941, ent_qs=[0.692, 0.951, 0.989, 0.997, 1.0]\n  bin0 (inv_dense q0-20):  size=4000, pct_at_cap=0.080, ent_mean=0.959, ent_qs=[0.753,\
    \ 0.980, 0.995, 0.998, 1.0]\n  bin1 (inv_dense q20-40): size=4000, pct_at_cap=0.170, ent_mean=0.934, ent_qs=[0.688, 0.936, 0.985, 0.997, 1.0]\n  bin2 (inv_dense q40-60): size=4000, pct_at_cap=0.220, ent_mean=0.921, ent_qs=[0.641, 0.906, 0.982, 0.995, 0.999]\n  bin3 (inv_dense q60-80): size=4000, pct_at_cap=0.183, ent_mean=0.933, ent_qs=[0.660, 0.934, 0.986, 0.995, 0.999]\n  bin4 (inv_dense q80-100):size=4000, pct_at_cap=0.091, ent_mean=0.957, ent_qs=[0.782, 0.978, 0.991, 0.998, 1.0]\n\n  Quantiles of inv_dense used for bins (approx): [0.0, 0.625047, 0.757647, 0.836440, 0.894220, 1.0]\n\n  Notes:\n  - Neighbor batch entropy is high overall and across density bands, consistent with strong multi-batch mixing even after symmetrization.\n  - Cap occupancy peaks in mid-density bins (q40–60 ≈0.220), lower in extremes (dense/sparse), suggesting moderate hubbing concentrated in mid densities.\n  - Together with directed-gap results (EID 1086), this indicates symmetrization disrupts density-coupled\
    \ per-cell targets, reducing corr(th,und), without necessarily introducing many purely within-batch inbound edges.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Inbound entropy @ ridge: high multi-batch mixing; sym disrupts density-coupling, not pure within-batch\"\ncontent: |\n  EID 1093 (test-only): inbound neighbor batch entropy and cap occupancy by density quintiles at the ridge.\n  Summary:\n  - Overall normalized neighbor batch entropy ≈0.941 (q=[0.692, 0.951, 0.989, 0.997, 1.000]); similarly high across all density quintiles.\n  - pct at inbound cap≈0.149 overall; peaks in mid-density bins (q40–60≈0.220; q60–80≈0.183), lower in dense/sparse extremes.\n  Interpretation update (with EID 1086 directed-gap):\n  - Directed quotas realize f_cross targets (corr(th,dir)≈0.984); undirected (symmetrized) lowers corr(th,und)≈0.139 and mean f_cross by ~0.033.\n  - High inbound entropy implies inbound neighbors remain multi-batch; the primary\
    \ issue is symmetrization weakening the density-coupled per-cell mapping, not simply adding within-batch edges.\n  Guidance:\n  - Avoid global quota re-centering. Keep ridge frozen; report diagnostics (directed-gap + inbound-entropy) alongside metrics.\n  - If revisiting, prefer narrow, diagnostics-led probes (e.g., density-banded analysis) with strict stop rules.\n  - Cross-organ plan unchanged: run frozen grid + paired baselines; replicate diagnostics.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write praxis/archive_addendum_appendix_entropy.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Appendix: Inbound neighbor batch entropy and cap occupancy by density (Heart ridge)\n  Settings: k=50, kd=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605, α_g=0.5, cap=2.0 (ridge). Test EID 1093 (test-only).\n\n  Results:\n  - Overall normalized neighbor batch entropy ≈0.941 (q=[0.692, 0.951, 0.989, 0.997, 1.000]).\n  - By inv_density quintiles:\n    • q0–20:  ent_mean≈0.959, pct_at_cap≈0.080\n    • q20–40: ent_mean≈0.934,\
    \ pct_at_cap≈0.170\n    • q40–60: ent_mean≈0.921, pct_at_cap≈0.220 (peak)\n    • q60–80: ent_mean≈0.933, pct_at_cap≈0.183\n    • q80–100:ent_mean≈0.957, pct_at_cap≈0.091\n  - Cap threshold=100 (2.0 × k_total); overall pct_at_cap≈0.149.\n\n  Interpretation (with directed-gap, EID 1086):\n  - Quotas realize theoretical f_cross in the directed graph; symmetrization lowers corr(th,und) and mean f_cross by ~0.033.\n  - High inbound entropy indicates neighbors remain multi-batch; the drop in corr reflects symmetrization weakening density-coupled per-cell targets rather than introducing many purely within-batch inbound edges.\n  Takeaway:\n  - Maintain frozen ridge; avoid global quota re-centering. Publish diagnostics alongside metrics; extend the same analysis to new organs.\n```\n\nNo pruning this turn; I’ll revisit pruning if/when token usage exceeds ~75% and will compress long reflective and iterative-probe ticks with concise, information-complete summaries."
thinking_content: "**Analyzing entropy and cross fraction**\n\nI realized that my cross fraction means 0.769 compared to a theoretical value of 0.802. The high entropy of around 0.94 aligns with a cross fraction of roughly 0.76, likely because, with four batches, maximum entropy occurs with a uniform distribution. With a cross fraction near 0.76, the within-batch share would be around 0.24, making the entropy of that distribution plausible. The small difference of 0.033 in values suggests a high degree of mixing, which impacts our previous interpretations of inbound edges and correlation.**Updating analysis and pruning**\n\nI need to be cautious with the correlation values. The directed correlation of nearly 0.984 suggests that f_dir tracks f_th on a cell-by-cell basis, while the undirected correlation of 0.139 indicates that inbound edges distort the per-cell cross fraction relative to the target. It appears that these inbound edges are not predominantly within-batch; they have high entropy\
  \ but are misaligned with theoretical coupling. We should revise our interpretation accordingly. \n\nIn terms of token management, with 42% usage, I recommend not pruning just yet. Instead, let's create a list of potential blocks to prune once we hit 75%.**Deciding on pruning actions**\n\nThe document mentions using pruning actions only when usage is over 75%, but it's not a strict rule. There's a note about cooldown for pruning actions, but no specific value is provided, which is a bit unclear. Since we’re not close to 270k usage, I think it might be wise to hold off on pruning to avoid overloading the system. Instead, I’ll focus on researching and diagnosing inbound results. \n\nI’ll store the inbound entropy logs as indicated, using the printed output from System Messages.**Summarizing inbound entropy results**\n\nI need to create a capsule in private memory summarizing the inbound entropy results and updating interpretations, noting that symmetric reductions affect coupling correlation\
  \ while inbound entropy stays high. The highest capacity occupancy is at mid-density, indicating it controls hubbing without adjusting quotas.\n\nNext, I’ll move to research to either update the existing archive_addendum_appendix_directed_gap.md or create a new file called archive_addendum_appendix_entropy.md for the summary. I'll include the test ID 1093 in the appendix reference.\n\nIt’s also important to plan for future cross-organ analysis. I’ll make sure to be precise when crafting notes, especially with YAML formatting, remembering to use quotes around titles that contain colons.**Creating actions and summaries**\n\nI realize we can request \"review 1093,\" but since it’s just a test, and we have the logs already, I’ll skip it and focus on creating our actions.\n\nFirst, I’ll structure the content for storage write to the specified file path. It includes detailed inbound entropy by density quintiles, using both approximate and exact numbers.\n\nNext, I’ll create a private memory\
  \ capsule titled \"Inbound entropy @ ridge: high mixing across batches; sym reduces density-coupling,\" summarizing key findings and interpretations.\n\nFinally, I need to update the archive with 'praxis/archive_addendum_appendix_entropy.md,' and I’ll decide to leave Token Management without pruning for now at 42% usage. I'll ensure YAML quoting for titles throughout."
