tick: 713
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis IV, in the Research Counter. Recap: Diagnostics indicate the directed quotas match theoretical mixing; symmetrization reduces the realized mixing, while inbound entropy is high across density quintiles. For completeness of the analysis addendum, I will run a synergy diagnostic comparing DAQB vs vanilla kNN on Zcorr: neighbor-persistence (Jaccard) and cross-batch fraction medians.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write praxis/diagnostics_synergy_test.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n\n  import numpy as np\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  # Utilities (inline to avoid import fragility)\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum,\
    \ inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd.astype(np.float32, copy=False))\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca'].astype(np.float32)\n\n  def _fw_weight_by_var(Xd: np.ndarray, alpha: float) -> np.ndarray:\n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1).astype(np.float32) + 1e-8\n      w = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha).astype(np.float32)\n      return Xd * w\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=np.float32)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(np.float32)\n      return B, cats\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=np.float32)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB,\
    \ B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def _symmetrize_binary(rows, cols, n):\n      A = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones_like(rows, dtype=np.float32), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      A_sym = A.maximum(A.T)\n      A_sym.eliminate_zeros()\n      return A_sym.tocsr()\n\n  def _neighbors_knn(Z: np.ndarray, k: int = 50, metric: str = 'cosine'):\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k+1, metric=metric, algorithm='brute')\n      nn.fit(Z)\n      d, idx = nn.kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n      rows, cols = [], []\n      for i in range(n):\n          nbrs = idx[i, 1:]  # drop self\n          rows.extend([i]*k)\n          cols.extend(nbrs.tolist())\n      rows = np.asarray(rows, dtype=np.int32)\n      cols = np.asarray(cols, dtype=np.int32)\n      A_sym = _symmetrize_binary(rows, cols, n)\n      return A_sym\n\n  def _cbf(C: sp.csr_matrix, bc: np.ndarray):\n      C = C.tocsr()\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      frac = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.float32)\n      for i in range(n):\n\
    \          s, e = C.indptr[i], C.indptr[i+1]\n          if e <= s:\n              frac[i] = 0.0\n              continue\n          neigh = C.indices[s:e]\n          frac[i] = np.mean(bc[neigh] != bc[i]).astype(np.float32)\n      return frac\n\n  def _median_jaccard(C1: sp.csr_matrix, C2: sp.csr_matrix):\n      C1 = C1.tocsr(); C2 = C2.tocsr()\n      n = C1.shape[0]\n      j = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.float32)\n      for i in range(n):\n          s1, e1 = C1.indptr[i], C1.indptr[i+1]\n          s2, e2 = C2.indptr[i], C2.indptr[i+1]\n          if e1 <= s1 and e2 <= s2:\n              j[i] = 1.0\n              continue\n          set1 = set(C1.indices[s1:e1].tolist())\n          set2 = set(C2.indices[s2:e2].tolist())\n          inter = len(set1.intersection(set2))\n          union = max(len(set1) + len(set2) - inter, 1)\n          j[i] = inter / union\n      return float(np.median(j)), float(np.mean(j))\n\n  def test(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      print(\"[Synergy diagnostics] ridge vs vanilla\
    \ kNN on Zcorr\")\n      # Preprocess\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      bc_codes = adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').cat.codes.to_numpy().astype(np.int32)\n\n      # Graph path residualization (GraphFWPCA α_g=0.5)\n      Xw_graph = _fw_weight_by_var(Xd, alpha=0.5)\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xw_graph, n_comps=50, random_state=0)\n      B, cats = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=1e-3)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0).astype(np.float32)\n      lam = 0.605\n      gamma\
    \ = np.minimum(0.74, lam * R2).astype(np.float32)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma[None, :])\n\n      # Vanilla kNN on Zcorr (all-batch)\n      C_knn = _neighbors_knn(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), k=50, metric='cosine')\n\n      # DAQB at ridge\n      from praxis.bbsg_density_adaptive import build_density_adaptive_bbsg\n      C_daqb, _ = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches,\n                                              k_total=50, metric='cosine',\n                                              delta=0.10, k_density=22, rng_seed=0)\n\n      # Metrics: Jaccard and cross-batch fraction medians\n      j_med, j_mean = _median_jaccard(C_knn, C_daqb)\n      cbf_knn = _cbf(C_knn, bc_codes)\n      cbf_daqb = _cbf(C_daqb, bc_codes)\n      print(f\"Jaccard(C_knn, C_daqb): median={j_med:.3f}, mean={j_mean:.3f}\")\n      print(f\"Cross-batch fraction median: kNN={np.median(cbf_knn):.3f}, DAQB={np.median(cbf_daqb):.3f}\")\n      return {\n          'jaccard_median':\
    \ j_med,\n          'jaccard_mean': j_mean,\n          'cbf_knn_median': float(np.median(cbf_knn)),\n          'cbf_daqb_median': float(np.median(cbf_daqb)),\n      }\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Synergy @ Ridge (test-only): kNN on Zcorr vs DAQB — Jaccard and cross-batch medians\"\ntags: \"diagnostics, synergy, ridge, knn, daqb\"\nabstract: \"Compares vanilla kNN on Zcorr to DAQB at ridge via neighbor-persistence (Jaccard) and cross-batch fraction medians.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from diagnostics_synergy_test import test as _test\n\n  def test(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      return _test(adata)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating changes to Graph_conn**\n\nI’m cautious about changing anything that could cause errors or degrade Graph_conn — that feels risky. The guidelines say we should avoid drastically altering identity. Maybe I can propose a cross-organ plan to execute when it's available, but I'm limited to the heart dataset for this task.\n\nI could refine my analysis by running sample-level neighbor persistence Jaccard between kNN and DAQB to explore synergistic mechanisms. But I also have some appendices already produced. So, I need to consider whether to prune older tokens for efficiency; it's at 42%, so not necessary yet. It’s important to correct my earlier error regarding writing storage files in the token management room.**Planning next steps for SOTA**\n\nI'm thinking about preparing a single \"confirmatory seed\" run for SOTA with the same parameters but fewer random elements. We've already had one seed with a score of 0.7414, while SOTA is at 0.7465. I could try replicating\
  \ the exact SOTA runner since it's stable.\n\nAnother idea is to consider a minor variant with kd=22 and k=50, keeping δ=0.10 as the best. Although I'd want to touch gamma_max slightly, it’s fixed at 0.74, and micro-sweeps may not be necessary unless there's a strong signal. Our diagnostics suggest symmetry undermines correlation — perhaps symmetrization by intersection could help. However, that might affect Graph_conn negatively. For now, I think I’ll finalize the addendum and documentation instead.**Organizing research resources**\n\nI can check our research storage for default documents and share the diagnostics tool code in a common area for future use. However, matured agents might only be able to read that. The shared storage is accessible to everyone, which is good.\n\nIt could be helpful to write a small utilities module for running diagnostics easily. I’ve already updated our ridge entries to include inbound entropy and directed-gap references, so that’s taken care of.\n\nWe could\
  \ prepare a \"public memory\" post once matured, but that's not available yet. I plan to set aside some time for the Reflection Chamber rather than rushing into action. We should compute synergy analysis using the script found in Capsule 43, as that exists in shared storage. It sounds like a good plan to print synergy stats with a test function.**Planning synergy analysis execution**\n\nI’m planning to wrap a test-only script to call for synergy analysis, which should help with further analysis. While adding multiple diagnostic test runs is allowed, they don't impact scores but do cost tokens. We're currently at 45%, so running the synergy analysis once seems fine for completeness.\n\nNext, I need to locate the script in storage at \"shared/diagnostics/graphfwpca_synergy_analysis.py\" and read it using /execute_action. Then, I can create a test harness to invoke it. However, writing my own synergy analysis function similar to \"daqb_diagnostics\" could also be a good approach. This would\
  \ involve comparing DAQB with vanilla kNN neighbor Jaccard and cross-batch medians. I have some previous synergy data to reference as well.**Implementing synergy test code**\n\nI plan to replicate a similar process by implementing a test-only script called \"diagnostics_synergy_test.py.\" This will involve building a kNN graph on Zcorr, potentially per-batch, and constructing a DAQB using our ridge builder. For each cell, I’ll compute Jaccard between neighbor sets and medians, then compare cross-batch fractions for DAQB versus kNN.\n\nI’ll also correlate GraphFWPCA weights with the between-batch ratio. However, since some steps seem complex, I’ll focus on calculating Jaccard and median metrics instead. \n\nNext, I'll code kNN using sklearn's NearestNeighbors, ensuring I comply with adjacency requirements while keeping the implementation straightforward. After finalizing the code, I’ll write it to storage, and submit it with test mode. I'll also monitor token usage, currently at 45%, which\
  \ is fine. Let’s move forward!"
