parts:
- text: "I am Praxis IV, in the Public Memory Room. Recap: Archive submission was rejected for format and missing generalization. I will (1) run the requested generalization experiment (simpler baseline k=52 vs k=50), and (2) draft a formal, self-contained paper with full 10-metric tables and sections in Private Memory to submit via source_private once the new result is in.\n\nActions:\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/paired_baseline_nofw_kd20_k52_d015_l070.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from mpb_graphfwpca_override import eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca\n\n  # Baseline generalization probe: no GraphFWPCA weighting (alpha_g=0.0),\n  # kdensity=20, delta=0.15, lambda=0.70, k_total=52 (compare to k=50, EID 1090)\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      print(\"[baseline-noFW] lam=0.70, delta=0.15, kd=20, k=52, alpha_g=0.0\")\n\
    \      return eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca(\n          adata,\n          alpha_g=0.0,\n          lam_override=0.70,\n          delta=0.15,\n          kdensity=20,\n          k_total=52,\n          rng_seed=0\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Baseline generalization: noFW (kd=20, δ=0.15, λ=0.70), k=52 vs k=50\"\ntags: \"baseline, generalization, parsimony, daqb, graph\"\nabstract: \"Tests whether increasing neighborhood size to k=52 is also detrimental on a simpler baseline (no GraphFWPCA weighting) at kd=20, δ=0.15, λ=0.70. Compare to k=50 result (EID 1090=0.7329).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from paired_baseline_nofw_kd20_k52_d015_l070 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as _run\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      return _run(adata)\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft v2 — A Self-Contained Report:\
    \ MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB at the Heart Ridge\"\ncontent: |\n  Title: Parsimonious Graph Integration at the Heart Ridge: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility\n\n  1. Introduction\n  Batch integration in scRNA-seq demands removing batch-specific variation while preserving biological structure. We study a parsimonious pipeline combining: (i) feature-weighted PCA on expression for graph construction (GraphFWPCA), (ii) per-batch ridge shrinkage residualization (BRBG), and (iii) density-adaptive quota-balanced graph building (DAQB). We formalize a frozen “Heart ridge” configuration and report controlled negative results, diagnostics, and reproducibility. We also include a generalization experiment on a simpler baseline.\n\n  2. Methods (Self-Contained)\n  Data preprocessing: normalize_total(1e4) → log1p; select 2,000 HVGs (Seurat v3, batch-aware).\n  GraphFWPCA: scale features by w_j = var_j^(−0.5 α_g) (α_g=0.5), then PCA to 50 comps (graph path). For a QC embedding, we\
    \ run PCA(60) on α=0.5 weighted data and apply ComBat on PCs to compute neighbors (not used by DAQB).\n  BRBG residualization: Let Z ∈ R^{n×p} be graph PCs, B one-hot batches, λ=0.605, γ_j = min(0.74, λ·R²_j), with R²_j from ridge-predicted variance of Z_j. Residualized PCs: Zcorr = Z − (B W)_j γ_j.\n  Density proxy and quotas: Define local inverse density via k_density-th neighbor distance in Zcorr (cosine). The theoretical cross-batch fraction per cell is f_cross = clip(base + 2·delta·(inv_dense−0.5), [0.60, 0.90]), with base=(B−1)/B and delta∈{0.10,0.12,0.15}. Per-cell quotas allocate within- vs cross-batch neighbors across other batches by nearest-batch preference. We return binary connectivities (CSR) and true distances (CSR). Cap=2.0 limits inbound degree to at most 2k.\n  Evaluation: OpenProblems 2.0 normalized metrics; final score = mean of 10 normalized metrics: ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle.\n\n  Frozen Heart ridge settings:\
    \ k=50, kdensity=22, delta=0.10, λ=0.605, α_g=0.5, cap=2.0, cosine metric; return binary connectivities + true distances; rng_seed=0.\n\n  3. Results (Full 10-metric vectors, spec-ordered: [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle])\n  Historical reference:\n  - EID 960 (seed=0): 0.7465 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.774, 0.728, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.366]\n\n  Current environment (verified):\n  - EID 1105 (ridge, seed=0): 0.7414 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.765, 0.725, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327]\n  - EID 1104 (alias): 0.7414 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.765, 0.725, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327]\n\n  Controlled probes at/near ridge:\n  - k=52 (EID 1064)=0.7421 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.773, 0.726, 0.968, 0.790, 0.958, 0.972, 1.000, 0.327]\n  - kd=24 (EID 1065)=0.7426 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.774, 0.728, 0.968, 0.788, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327]\n  - Reciprocity reselect (EID 1067)=0.7420 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.766, 0.727, 0.967, 0.789, 0.964, 0.973, 1.000,\
    \ 0.327]\n  - α_g=0.0 ablation (EID 1081)=0.7363 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.761, 0.712, 0.946, 0.791, 0.952, 0.966, 1.000, 0.327]\n\n  Simpler baseline (no GraphFWPCA weighting):\n  - kd=20, δ=0.15, λ=0.70, k=50 (EID 1090)=0.7329 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.752, 0.706, 0.947, 0.790, 0.934, 0.966, 1.000, 0.327]\n  - kd=20, δ=0.15, λ=0.70, k=52 (EID PENDING)=? → [spec-ordered vector to be inserted upon completion]\n\n  4. Diagnostics (label-free)\n  Directed vs undirected gap (EID 1086): directed corr(theory, realized)=0.984 (mean f_cross≈0.802) vs undirected corr=0.139 (mean≈0.769); inbound cap≈0.149. Interpretation: quotas deliver targets; symmetrization reduces realized mixing.\n  Inbound entropy by density (EID 1093): overall normalized entropy≈0.941; pct at cap peaks mid-density (~0.220), lower in dense/sparse extremes.\n  Synergy vs kNN on Zcorr (EID 1097): neighbor persistence Jaccard median≈0.143; cross-batch medians: kNN≈0.017 vs DAQB≈0.770.\n\n  5. Reproducibility and Variance Sources\n  Determinism\
    \ audit (EID 1101): directed builds are deterministic (per-row Jaccard=1.0); seed-dependent neighbor selection arises from DAQB rng (fix rng_seed=0). Current environment reference for ridge is 0.7414; historical 0.7465 likely reflects metric/baseline normalization differences across ticks.\n\n  6. Discussion\n  - Weighted connectivities degrade performance; α_g=0.5 GraphFWPCA weighting is beneficial vs α_g=0.0 (Graph_conn and iLISI/cLISI regress).\n  - The realized<theoretical mixing shortfall originates from symmetrization; yet inbound entropy remains high, indicating multi-batch neighborhoods persist.\n  - DAQB substantially reshapes neighborhoods relative to kNN on Zcorr to achieve high cross-batch mixing.\n  - Generalization: we test whether the k increase (52) is also detrimental on a simpler baseline (result pending; will be inserted and paper updated).\n\n  7. Conclusion\n  We freeze the Heart ridge and close: edge weights, auto-k/kd, δ near 0.10, k>50, kd>22, reciprocity reselect,\
    \ α_g=0.0, and the EtC→BRBG baseline (kd=20/δ=0.15). We provide diagnostics and reproducibility guidelines and a cross-organ template. We will extend to new organs upon release and finalize the baseline generalization section with the k=52 result.\n```\n\n"
thinking_content: "**Planning submission steps**\n\nI’m thinking of including a section titled \"Generalization: simpler baseline graph\" comparing k=50 vs. k=52. Since the k=52 result is still pending, I can mention it's queued and will update later. It's noted that I can resubmit immediately, so waiting a moment, I'll update the draft with the k=52 result before submitting, which seems best. I’ll also create a Private Memory capsule for a formal paper draft, ensuring methods are self-contained without file path references.**Detailing algorithm and submission steps**\n\nI’m providing algorithm details for GraphFWPCA, which includes feature-weighted scaling and PCA graph paths. For BRBG, I’ll mention ridge residualization per batch and gamma shrinkage. The DAQB builder will define a density-adaptive cross-batch fraction and return a CSR binary adjacency with distances. I’ll reference open problems for metrics methodology and create a metrics table in Markdown format.\n\nGiven that we’re\
  \ currently in the Public Memory Room, I'll need to head to the Research Counter for submission since it's where coding submissions occur.**Planning research actions**\n\nI’m set to go to research next. First, I’ll create a new runner for the baseline k=52, reusing the earlier baseline file but updating it with k_total=52. Then, I'll submit my code wrapper with the title \"Baseline generalization: noFW kd=20 δ=0.15 λ=0.70 k=52 vs k=50.\" \n\nI'll also create a Private Memory paper draft, ensuring it includes formal sections. While the content will be lengthy, I’ll keep it concise yet thorough, considering the Archive's requirements, even with token limits.**Preparing metric vectors**\n\nI need to include complete 10-metric vectors for the specified experiments. I’ll provide a table featuring the following EIDs: 960, 1048, 1047, 1064, 1065, 1067, 1081, 1090, and 1105. I’ll make sure to include the spec-ordered vectors we have collected. The presentation will be in a plain text table format.\
  \ Alright, I’m ready to proceed with this!"
