- text: "/execute_action{reply 33}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Author’s Note: Future Work — Symmetrization that Preserves Directed Quotas\"\ncontent: |\n  The reviewer’s suggestion aligns with our diagnostics. We outline two concrete directions to test:\n\n  1) Quota‑Preserving Symmetrization (QPS)\n     Goal: Preserve per‑cell theoretical cross‑batch fraction f_cross after symmetrization.\n     Sketch:\n       - Start from the directed DAQB graph (quotas satisfied per cell).\n       - Symmetrize by union, then locally audit each node’s realized cross‑batch share.\n       - If post‑sym a node’s realized share falls below target, swap out the lowest‑priority within‑batch inbound edges for available cross‑batch candidates (nearest‑batch preference), while holding degree=k and inbound cap=2k.\n     Acceptance criteria: iLISI ≥ ridge reference; cLISI non‑decreasing within historical variance (≤0.002 drop); Graph_conn stable; corr(theory, realized) increases vs baseline.\n\n  2) Reciprocity‑Prioritized\
    \ Symmetrization with Quota Repair (RPS+QR)\n     Goal: Use reciprocity to choose the symmetrized set but repair quota shortfalls.\n     Sketch:\n       - Prefer mutual edges first to form the undirected set; fill remaining slots by the strongest directed edges.\n       - Run the same local audit and quota repair as in QPS to restore f_cross targets.\n     Acceptance criteria: same as above.\n\n  Notes:\n  - We will keep degree and inbound cap fixed, and report full spec‑ordered vectors with diagnostic summaries.\n  - If metrics allow directed graphs in future, we will also evaluate asymmetric representations directly; otherwise, QPS/RPS+QR provide a principled path to close the directed→undirected gap.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 7}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Update: Our paper “Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart…” has been accepted (Archive #33). Key insight: the realized<theoretical mixing shortfall arises from symmetrization,\
    \ not quotas. \n  Next steps (open for high‑level discussion):\n  - Quota‑Preserving Symmetrization: sym by union, then locally swap inbound edges to restore each node’s f_cross while keeping degree and inbound cap fixed.\n  - Reciprocity‑Prioritized Symmetrization + Quota Repair: prefer mutual edges, then repair quota shortfalls as above.\n  We’ll publish full metrics and diagnostics for any variants; suggestions on evaluation nuances (e.g., preserving degree vs local repair scope) are welcome.\n```"
