- text: "I am Praxis IV, at the External Counter. Recap: Heart ridge frozen; Archive #33 accepted; QPS symmetrization negative; docs updated. I need an administrative clarification about evaluation normalization/versioning and adjacency handling, and I’ll also snapshot the current baseline file from system storage.\n\n/execute_action{request_human}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Metric normalization drift and env/version tagging\"\ncontent: |\n  Context:\n  - Historical ridge (EID 960, tick 616) scored 0.7465 with vector [0.659, 0.248, 0.774, 0.728, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.366].\n  - Current environment reproductions (EIDs 1104/1105, seed=0) score 0.7414 with vector [0.659, 0.248, 0.765, 0.725, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327].\n  - The delta concentrates in ARI/NMI and Cell_cycle. Pipeline determinism is verified; DAQB rng is fixed at seed=0.\n  \n  Requests:\n  1) Please confirm whether the normalization baselines used for OpenProblems metrics (system/openproblems_baselines.json)\
    \ changed between tick 616 and now, and if so, provide:\n     - The previous vs current baseline file hashes or version IDs\n     - A short note on what changed (metrics, control methods, or normalization min/max)\n  2) Please confirm whether the evaluation pipeline symmetrizes obsp['connectivities'] and/or obsp['distances'] before computing metrics, or otherwise transforms them (e.g., re-running neighbors from obsm['X_emb']). If any symmetrization or recomputation is applied, could you share the relevant policy?\n  3) Recommended practice: Should we cite an “environment/version” identifier with each evaluation to ensure cross-tick comparability? If so, please advise on the canonical identifier (e.g., baseline file hash + metrics commit ID) and whether the station can add it to evaluation outputs.\n  \n  Rationale:\n  - We will keep our ridge entry and release notes accurate and include environment/version tags.\n  - This will help other agents interpret cross-tick score differences\
    \ without conflating them with algorithmic changes.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage read system/openproblems_baselines.json}"
thinking_content: '**Planning parallel activities**


  I think we should continue with activities in parallel, although the instructions mention proceeding with usual activities. We might not need to run more experiments right now. After the user''s request, I could return to the Research Counter to finish other tasks. Sure, I could do nothing else, but maybe it''s a good time to update our ridge entry to include an environment version placeholder for tracking. Still, I want to focus on the user''s request first.**Producing action request**


  I need to execute an action request with YAML content that includes a title about "Metric normalization drift and env versioning." The content will detail the differences between EID 960 and 1105 along with raw normalized scores. I’ll ask for clarification on baseline normalization differences, the current baseline file version, and best practices for citing environment across ticks. I''ll also propose adding an environment version stamp and check whether directed graph acceptance can be enabled. Plus, I want to clarify if evaluation metrics are computed on a symmetrized graph or directed connectivity.**Clarifying evaluation metrics**


  The system uses openproblems metrics, and for fairness, I want to find out whether evaluation functions symmetrize obsp[''connectivities'']. I can''t directly read system files from the storage path, but I can execute an action to retrieve ''system/openproblems_metrics.py'' to confirm this. Asking the user helps too, but I can investigate myself. I''ll ask if evaluation uses symmetrized adjacency for metrics and confirm the metrics version. Since I''m at the External Counter, accessing ''openproblems_baselines.json'' might be a better immediate action as well. I can include both options for my actions in one response.**Proposing action request**


  I want to propose executing an action that requests information about "Metric normalization drift and environment/version tagging." The YAML will include details about EID 960 and EID 1105 results, asking to confirm if baseline min/max values changed and the environment code version. I''d suggest adding an environment version and baseline hash to evaluation outputs. Additionally, I need to verify if adjacency is symmetrized for metric computation and how metrics are computed on obsp connectivities. After that, I plan to check relevant files in the Research Counter, but I might not have permission to read them.**Planning storage actions**


  I see that we can perform storage actions to read system files, specifically ''system/openproblems_baselines.json'' and metrics. Reading large files might consume a lot of tokens, but I still have 169k tokens left. While entire metrics files might add thousands of tokens, I can focus on just the baselines JSON first, which is likely smaller. Instead of attempting to read everything, I could ask the user for guidance and proceed with just the essentials. I''ll also craft a concise YAML request, remembering to quote the title correctly.'
