tick: 780
role: model
parts:
- text: "Reflection tick 1\n\n1) Potentially wrong assumptions at the ridge\n- Fixed k_total: Assuming a single neighborhood size fits all densities may be suboptimal. Signals: Graph_conn often limits; sparse regions likely need a few more neighbors while dense cores may need fewer to avoid local crowding.\n- Quota shape and clipping [0.60, 0.90]: The hard clip might be too aggressive or miscentered for other organs. Our directed-gap shows quotas are realized pre-sym; mis-specified targets could still bias mixing/Graph_conn trade-offs.\n- Density proxy = k_density-th NN distance: May not capture local reachability in anisotropic manifolds; could mis-rank density bands that drive quota allocation.\n- BRBG linearity (γ = min(γmax, λ·R²)): Linear mapping from batch-predictability to shrinkage might over- or under-correct specific PCs; slight nonlinearities (tempered exponents) could preserve cell-cycle/ARI better.\n- Feature-weighting prior (variance^−0.5·α_g): BAFW showed a tiny gain; variance-only\
    \ weighting may still overweight batch-predictable genes in some organs.\n- Cap uniformity (cap=2.0): Uniform inbound cap may concentrate hubbing in mid-density bands (observed). Cap scheduling by density might help Graph_conn without harming cLISI.\n\n2) Two principled, low-coupling probes for a new organ\n- Probe A: Density-adaptive k_total micro-schedule\n  - Change: k_total(i) = 50 + η·sign(inv_dense(i)−median) with η=+2 (sparse) and −2 (dense); keep per-cell quotas proportional to k_total(i) so expected cross-batch counts scale consistently.\n  - Hypothesis: Slightly larger k in sparse zones improves Graph_conn; slightly smaller k in dense cores avoids redundancy—net score ≥ baseline with cLISI unchanged.\n  - Metrics/stop: Require Graph_conn ≥ baseline and cLISI drop ≤ 0.002; stop if overall score < baseline by 0.001.\n- Probe B: Quota clip micro-adjustment\n  - Change: Replace clip [0.60, 0.90] with a slightly narrower, gentler target [0.62, 0.88] (same δ); no other changes.\n\
    \  - Hypothesis: Softer extremes reduce over-mixing at very dense/sparse bands, stabilizing Graph_conn without losing iLISI; ARI/NMI may improve modestly.\n  - Metrics/stop: Require no cLISI harm (≤ 0.002), Graph_conn not worse by > 0.003, overall ≥ baseline by ≥ 0.001; otherwise close.\n\nBoth probes are single-parameter tweaks, orthogonal to BRBG and DAQB logic, and should generalize or falsify fixed-k and fixed-clip assumptions without overfitting to Heart.\n\n3) Publication cadence and adoption thresholds for BAFW\n- Cadence: Maintain one substantive paper/update per ≤100 ticks. For new organs, publish an addendum once frozen ridge and diagnostics are replicated; include any BAFW results as a subsection.\n- BAFW adoption threshold (cross-organ):\n  - Evidence on ≥2 distinct organs: mean gain ≥ +0.003 over each organ’s ridge reference, with cLISI change ≥ −0.001 and Graph_conn ≥ baseline − 0.002.\n  - Seed robustness: Across seeds {0,1}, mean gain remains ≥ +0.002 with the same cLISI/Graph_conn\
    \ guards.\n  - If thresholds met, elevate BAFW(β=0.50) to recommended variant; otherwise keep as auxiliary and report as such."
thinking_content: "**Exploring low-coupling strategies**\n\nI'm considering low-coupling, which means ensuring minimal changes and easy interpretation. For instance, I could explore a density-proportional k_total schedule, perhaps testing fixed k to see if it limits results. Testing different per-cell quotas with options like clipping can be helpful, alongside using local reachability distance (LRD) compared to k-th neighbor distance. I want to keep testing while maintaining low coupling by changing only one parameter slightly. This ensures clarity and helps falsify assumptions effectively.**Proposing probes for exploration**\n\nI’m proposing two probes. \n\nProbe A focuses on a density-adaptive k_total with constant expected cross-batch neighbors. I could implement k_total(i) as k0*(1 + eta*(inv_dense(i)-median)), using a small eta, like 0.2. The idea here is that larger k in sparser regions can enhance Graph_conn without negatively impacting cLISI, which helps falsify the fixed-k assumption.\n\
  \nProbe B examines the quota shape and clipping bounds. I want to test options like [0.65, 0.90] or [0.55, 0.85], adjusting bounds while maintaining a delta of 0.10. Exploring narrower clips could clarify the iLISI/Graph_conn trade-off. I could instead test BRBG linearity, but that may introduce more coupling. Another low-coupling adjustment could be increasing Graph PCA dimensions from 50 to 56, although that might be closed off.**Evaluating density proxy and proposals**\n\nI need to evaluate the density proxy by adjusting the k_density neighbor order from kd=22 to be adaptive or based on local variance. I've tested kd changes with similar results. For low-coupling, I'll minimize feature weight to penalize highly batch-predictable PCs, possibly in Graph-PC weighting post-PCA, which might complicate things a bit.\n\nGiven constraints, here are my proposals: \n\n1) A density-adaptive k_total micro-schedule allowing for k +/- 2 neighbors with an eta parameter. \n\n2) Micro-adjust quota floors/ceilings\
  \ to [0.58, 0.88] for a narrower range, reducing extremes. Alternatively, exploring a \"per-cell cap schedule\" off cap=2.0 could work, though previous tests at 1.8 and 2.2 had mixed results.**Proposing new organ analysis**\n\nThe question specifies a new organ to avoid overfitting to the Heart. I propose running experiments on this new organ with early stops and specific thresholds. \n\nFor the publication cadence and BAFW adoption, I suggest we only adopt it if we see improvements of at least +0.003 in two organs, without harming cLISI or reducing Graph_conn significantly below the baseline. The cadence would be one paper every 100 ticks, supplemented with diagnostics and code releases. \n\nWe'll need to reflect on initial assumptions, outline two probes with minimal changes, and clarify adoption thresholds as well.**Reflecting in the Chamber**\n\nIn the Reflection Chamber, I’ll focus on crafting clear text without commands. I need to address \"BRBG linearity,\" which might feel unnatural.\
  \ I propose testing a per-PC shrinkage temperature with p set to 0.85 using the formula gamma = min(gamma_max, lam * R2^p), keeping it as low-coupling within BRBG. \n\nThere's a previous mention of \"softpower\" in my storage that we have partially explored, but it's not recorded across the organ yet. Regarding \"quota independence across cells,\" this assumption could reduce global connectivity. I want to consider a minimal \"bridge bonus\" with necessary edges linking low-degree components, though implementation is complex and not proposed for the new organ. Sticking to two simple probes for clarity is best."
