    \ Otherwise close the per‑k line for that organ.\n  - Diagnostics: compare directed‑gap and inbound‑entropy to ensure mixing targets hold and gains aren’t driven by over‑mixing.\n\n- Probe B: Quota‑clip micro‑adjustment (shape falsification)\n  - Change: narrow clip from [0.60,0.90] → [0.62,0.88] (same δ, same kdensity) so extremes are softened; all else frozen.\n  - Hypothesis: If the hard clip is mis‑shaped, softening extremes reduces over‑mixing in very dense/sparse bands, stabilizing Graph_conn without harming iLISI; modest ARI/NMI uptick possible.\n  - Success criteria: overall ≥ baseline by ≥0.001; cLISI drop ≤0.002; Graph_conn ≥ baseline−0.002. Otherwise close line.\n  - Implementation: minimally parametrize the quota clip in the f_cross calculation (no other logic touched). Keep RNG and kdensity identical for fair attribution.\n\nOptional, still low‑coupling (only if A/B inconclusive)\n- Probe C: BRBG nonlinearity map γ = min(γ_max, λ·R²^p) with p∈{0.9,1.1}\n  - Hypothesis: slight\
    \ concavity/convexity might restore ARI/NMI on PCs with mid‑R² while preserving mixing; reject if cLISI harm or Graph_conn loss.\n  - Same guardrails as B; single parameter toggle.\n\nPublication cadence and BAFW adoption policy (cross‑organ)\n- Cadence: Publish an addendum per ≤100 ticks per organ set—include frozen ridge vectors/EIDs, diagnostics, and any falsification probes (A/B/C). For BAFW, include a small “Methods delta” box and complete 10‑metric tables.\n- BAFW adoption threshold (organ‑level and cross‑organ):\n  - Organ‑level: ≥+0.003 over that organ’s ridge with cLISI ≥ baseline−0.001 and Graph_conn ≥ baseline−0.002 (seeds 0 and 1 average ≥+0.002).\n  - Cross‑organ: observed on ≥2 distinct organs under the same guardrails. If met, promote BAFW(β=0.50) from auxiliary to recommended variant; otherwise keep as auxiliary and report negative or marginal results explicitly.\n- Logging: pre‑register success criteria, print spec vectors, env v4.1 metadata, RNG seeds, and diagnostics\
    \ summaries to avoid post‑hoc overfitting."
