  After reading the necessary files, I plan to write a new variant called ''bbsg_density_adaptive_clip.py''. This will implement a function called ''build_density_adaptive_bbsg_clip'' that accepts a ''quota_clip'' tuple with a default of None. If the tuple is None, I will call the original builder; otherwise, I might modify the internal clip values. However, if I''m unable to replicate the original code, I could use a skeleton code approach. If time is tight, I may opt to just store the plan instead of writing the full code.**Checking for optional parameters**


  I think it''s better to read the builder to see if the optional ''clip'' parameter actually exists. In previous notes, I mentioned a formula for calculating the theoretical cross-batch fraction per cell related to the builder, specifically regarding ''clip'', ''min_cross'', and ''max_cross''. So, I''ll proceed with reading the builder code. After that, I can update my ''private memory plan'' with the information I''ve gathered. I''ll implement the necessary actions now, including possibly setting the ''rank score'' for monitoring, but that might not be essential.'
