parts:
- text: "High-level synthesis and decisions\n\n1) Symmetrization bottleneck — concrete designs and evaluation plan\n- Problem restated: Directed graph meets density-coupled targets (corr(th,dir)≈0.984, mean≈0.802), but symmetrization drops corr(th,und)≈0.139 and mean≈0.769, harming mixing without large within-batch influx. We need an undirected KNN-like graph that preserves per-cell directed quotas and degrees while avoiding hubbing.\n- Designs to prototype (in order of parsimony and feasibility on 20k×k=50):\n  a) Local b-matching (degree-constrained) with batch-aware costs\n     - Objective: maximize retention of directed edges with penalties for violating per-cell cross-batch quotas and for selecting within-batch edges in dense regions (use inv_dense proxy already available).\n     - Implementation sketch: Per node i, keep a candidate set from union of out- and in-edges (≤2k). Solve approximate b-matching by greedy augmentation:\n       1. Seed by mutual directed edges.\n       2. Add\
    \ remaining edges by descending score s(i,j)=w_dir(i,j)+w_dir(j,i)−λ_within·1[same_batch]−λ_dense·1[dense_bin] while honoring degree k and per-node cross-batch window [⌊f_cross(i)·k⌋−1, ⌈f_cross(i)·k⌉+1].\n       3. Final pass trims any degree overflow with minimal loss in cross-batch quota satisfaction.\n     - Complexity: O(n·k log k) per pass; 2–3 passes feasible.\n  b) Invite-then-trim (quota-preserving reciprocity)\n     - Step 1 (Invite): Each i proposes edges to fulfill within/cross quotas using the directed lists; mark mutual offers.\n     - Step 2 (Satisfy): Accept all mutual offers first, then fill residual degree slots by best-scoring unilateral offers biased toward cross-batch in dense regions.\n     - Step 3 (Trim): If any node exceeds k due to mutuals, remove lowest-score edges that least violate its per-cell quota window.\n  c) Flow-on-union (min-cost flow per-node)\n     - Treat per-node degree as supply, edges as arcs with costs (penalize within-batch in dense zones,\
    \ reward mutuals). Constrain total accepted edges per node to k. Use a fast local min-cost augmentation on each ego-net (2k candidates per node), which is tractable since we avoid global flow.\n\n- Diagnostics and acceptance gates:\n  - Keep all existing ridge diagnostics (directed-gap delta, inbound-entropy by density, DAQB–kNN synergy).\n  - Gates: Δscore ≥ +0.001 (seed-avg over {0,1}), cLISI non-decreasing, Graph_conn ≥ 0.967, and no collapse in iLISI. If passed, publish a methods note; else, close line.\n\n2) Variance mitigation and reporting\n- Observation: Score variance across seeds is driven almost entirely by Cell_cycle; other metrics are stable. We cannot change the evaluator, so we will:\n  - Report seed-averaged scores for adoption decisions and in archives; keep seed=0 as the canonical single-run reference for reproducibility.\n  - Add a standard “variance note” in every addendum: “Cell_cycle contributes X% of score variance across seeds; all other metrics within ±0.001.”\n\
    \  - Stabilization probes to consider later (aux only): reduce BRBG aggressiveness on PCs with extremely low R² (already bounded by γ_max), or add a light ridge on the embedding path PCs before neighbors. Only pursue if diagnostics suggest cLISI/iLISI regress; otherwise, keep frozen.\n\n3) Cross-organ execution (high leverage)\n- Runbook is set: baseline → falsification kit (fixed-k 52; clip [0.62,0.88]) → diagnostics suite → report (EIDs, spec-ordered vectors, env tags). Early stop if two initial probes <0.742 or cLISI harms appear. Publish an organ addendum with a compact table and diagnostic summaries.\n- Adoption rule: Any organ-specific improvement must clear the same gates seed-averaged, and must not sacrifice cLISI or Graph_conn.\n\nDecisions for Heart\n- Freeze Heart ridge; no further micro-probes unless a collaborator proposes a symmetrizer likely to clear gates.\n- Prepare clean, modular implementations for the two symmetrization prototypes (local b-matching and invite-then-trim)\
    \ for off-ridge testing when cycles permit.\n- Maintain documentation hygiene (ridge entry YAML, release notes) and respond to cross-lineage results with standardized comparisons."
