tick: 816
role: model
parts:
- text: "Deeper analysis and experiment design\n\nA) Quantifying where +0.001 must come from\n- Empirical deltas from Anscombe@ridge (seed0): ΔASW_label≈+0.029, ΔcLISI≈+0.004, ΔGraph_conn≈−0.004, ΔkBET≈−0.019, ΔiLISI≈−0.002, others ~flat. Net ≈ +0.00035.\n- Implication: to clear +0.001, we likely need at least one of:\n  - Recover kBET by ~+0.01 without hurting cLISI, or\n  - Raise iLISI by ~+0.005–0.006, or\n  - Improve Graph_conn by ~+0.002–0.003, while keeping ASW_label gains.\n- Diagnostic target-setting: use the baselines’ normalization ranges to compute a “marginal gain budget” per metric; prioritize probes that add iLISI/cLISI or recover Graph_conn/kBET when ASW_label is nudged upward.\n\nB) Why symmetrization lowers realized mixing (mechanism sketch)\n- Asymmetry resolution is biased: low-density nodes (or those with fewer incoming offers) “fill” degrees with within-batch neighbors because they lack sufficient cross-batch mutuals; high-density nodes already satisfy quotas but lose\
    \ some cross edges when symmetry is enforced (degree trims).\n- Per-node degree k constraint interacts with per-node f_cross; when transforming to undirected, we implicitly solve a matching problem with soft quota constraints. Greedy mutual-first + degree capping tends to reduce per-node cross-batch targets in dense regions; the shortfall shows up as corr(th,und)↓ and mean f_cross↓.\n\nC) Two minimal symmetrizers worth building (off-ridge prototypes)\n- Mutual-first + quota-window greedy (invite-then-trim lite)\n  - Start with mutual directed edges; maintain for each node i a window Wi=[⌊f_cross(i)k⌋−1, ⌈f_cross(i)k⌉+1].\n  - Fill residual degrees with unilateral offers ranked by s(i,j)=mutual_flag·β + cross_flag·(γ1 + γ2·inv_dense(i)) − within_flag·γ3, subject to staying inside Wi as much as possible.\n  - If degree > k, drop lowest-score edges that least violate Wi and that are within-batch in mid/high density bins.\n  - Expected effect: retain most directed cross edges in dense cores\
    \ while giving sparse nodes a quota-respecting fill.\n- Local b-matching (degree-constrained) with batch-aware costs\n  - Candidate set: union of in/out directed edges (≤2k per node).\n  - Greedy augmentation under degree k with cost c(i,j)=−(mutual_bonus) − (cross_bonus·inv_dense(i)) + (within_penalty·density_bin).\n  - One or two passes suffice; far cheaper than global min-cost flow; preserves per-node mixing windows approximately.\n\nEvaluation: apply ridge suite (directed-gap delta, inbound entropy, DAQB–kNN synergy) and gates (Δscore≥+0.001, cLISI non-decreasing, Graph_conn≥0.967, seed-avg).\n\nD) Seed variance triage (Cell_cycle)\n- Observation: Across seeds, score swings are dominated by Cell_cycle; other metrics are stable. Likely causes:\n  - Slightly different DAQB neighbor realizations change the mixing of cell cycle–related neighborhoods, affecting Cell_cycle preservation metric.\n  - BRBG gamma mapping already caps extremes (γ_max); additional stabilization may not improve\
    \ net score if it trades off cLISI/iLISI.\n- Policy: Report seed-averaged results for adoption; cite a “variance note” quantifying Cell_cycle contribution. Only revisit stabilization if diagnostics show systemic iLISI/cLISI regressions under new organs.\n\nE) Cross-organ plan (why it matters)\n- Heart is saturated for ridge-style probes; most changes reallocate gains between ASW_label/cLISI and Graph_conn/kBET near zero net.\n- New organ may have different density/mixing structure; the falsification kit can quickly verify ridge robustness and expose organ-specific levers (e.g., δ sensitivity, kdensity parity).\n- Publishing organ addenda with standardized tables/diagnostics will provide more value than more Heart micro-sweeps.\n\nF) Documentation hygiene and collaboration\n- Keep ridge entry YAML authoritative with env tags, EIDs, full vectors.\n- Encourage peers to report seed-averaged results and to run the suite; unify acceptance gates to avoid noisy “improvements.”\n- If collaborators\
    \ deliver a promising symmetrizer, evaluate under our suite and gates; co-author a short methods note if it clears thresholds."
thinking_content: "**Evaluating contribution metrics**\n\nI'm diving into evaluation contributions by calculating the overall score mean of normalized metrics. It looks like a +0.029 increase in ASW_label versus a -0.004 in Graph_conn gives a net delta that's roughly neutral after considering other values. To achieve even a +0.001 improvement, I realize I need significant enhancements across multiple metrics. I'll propose focusing on metrics with large ranges because boosting those could provide more benefits, especially with a \"marginal gain budget\" to evaluate proposals.**Exploring kBET sensitivity**\n\nI'm thinking about the hypothesis that kBET sensitivity might come from global neighbor share compared to local quotas, and I'm wondering if I could adjust δ. However, since δ micro-sweeps are closed, maybe I should consider combining normalization with a slight increase in δ to 0.11. Earlier experiments with δ near 0.1 had parity, but I want to keep it simple. \n\nI’ll also propose analyzing\
  \ which principal components are correlated with the cell cycle, as well as examining how gamma suppression could impact residualization along cell-cycle-similar neighborhoods.**Planning reflection and experiments**\n\nI think it's important to keep reflection and produce detailed bullet points without executing any actions just yet. For reflection, I need to focus on a deliverable that provides deeper insights. I’m proposing experiments like seed stratification and deterministic analysis, along with \"jackknife\" metrics. It seems beneficial to compute the sensitivity of scores to each metric using baseline minimum and maximum values. Plus, I want to propose a gradient for improvement and suggest storing an algorithmic skeleton for symmetry. Let’s move forward with these ideas!"
