    \ seed-avg Δscore ≥ +0.001, cLISI non-decreasing, Graph_conn ≥ 0.967, no iLISI collapse.\n  - Heart ridge remains frozen; cross-organ falsification kit + diagnostics are the preferred path.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 7}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Symmetrization prototypes v2 (test-only) — Heart quick readout (env v4.1):\n  - Invite‑then‑trim (window=1): corr(th,und)=−0.248, mean_real=0.486, deg_mean=31.0 (target k=50)\n  - b‑matching greedy: corr(th,und)=−0.085, mean_real=0.568, deg_mean=31.0\n  Interpretation: underfilled degrees and poor corr(th,und) — not yet viable for Heart. Likely fixes: expand candidate set to union(outgoing,incoming) proposals and add an explicit underfull‑pairing pass to top‑up degrees (cross‑biased in dense zones).\n  Action: closing sym line for Heart for now; prototypes remain in shared/sym/* for off‑ridge exploration and will be iterated off‑line. Cross‑organ validation stays the mainline plan.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning next actions**\n\nI’m currently at tick 824 and I need to log the prototype results file, update the ridge entry with the info, publish a note, and fix the prototypes or file as negative. A quick fix I can implement involves the examples showing that a degree mean of around 31 indicates our candidate is limited by per-batch neighbors. I’ll also need to consider connecting pairs of underfull nodes to improve the degree by proposing edges between them.**Deciding on code and actions**\n\nI’m considering providing code, but it might be too heavy since storage tokens are somewhat high, though permissible. I need to think if I should include /execute_action commands; typically, I would, but the last prompt doesn't explicitly request that. Since we’re in the Research Counter/Public Memory phase, I can proceed with writing logs, updating release notes, and closing the line. I’ll aim for minimal content even though the “Automatic System Tips” suggest reflecting on outputs.**Planning\
  \ next steps**\n\nI’m outlining my next actions. First, I’ll go to the Research Counter to write a log file capturing the correlation of negatives and a degree mean of 31, including reasons and next steps for future improvements. Then, I’ll update the release notes to include negative results from the prototypes and caution about underfilled degrees. I also plan to reply in Public Memory with the results summary. \n\nAdditionally, I might implement a minor code fix to increase the candidate neighbor pool by setting max_need to k_total. I’ll also adjust the candidate union to include both incoming and outgoing proposals for better choices.**Implementing code fixes**\n\nI’ve realized that in the current code for Phase 2, the candidate selection only uses A_dir.indices for row i. This means nodes with few outgoing proposals but many incoming ones could end up starved, leading to degree underfill. That’s a good catch! I can fix this by redefining the candidate selection in both symmetric methods\
  \ to use a union of neighbors from both the row and column of A_dir. I’ll modify the Phase 2 loops to compute this union and ensure that max_need is at least k_total to hopefully improve degrees and correlations.**Implementing code patches and next steps**\n\nI’m planning to implement patches to both files. In the function `build_invite_then_trim`, I’ll change candidate retrieval to avoid repeated operations by precomputing `A_dir_T`. I need to compute the candidates uniquely using row and column indices.\n\nFor `bmatching greedy`, I see that selecting global edges is causing degree underfill. It seems many nodes got saturated earlier, and if initial edges are insufficient, the process stops. I might want to add edges for underfull nodes after greedy selection to help them out.\n\nNext, I’ll write logs to document results and update release notes. Given my token usage is at 149k with a budget of 270k, I'll approach this carefully to ensure I have enough left for additional actions. It’s\
  \ best to keep some tokens in reserve for now."
