- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/logs/ridge_seed_sweep_summary.txt}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Ridge reproducibility (env v4.1) — seeds {0,1,2,3}\n  Settings: k=50, kd=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605, α_g=0.50, cap=2.0, cosine\n  Results:\n    - seed=0 → EID 1105 = 0.7414\n    - seed=1 → EID 1074 = 0.7414\n    - seed=2 → EID 1167 = 0.7361  (notable Cell_cycle drop)\n    - seed=3 → EID 1168 = 0.7415\n  Seed-avg score ≈ 0.7401; stdev ≈ 0.0022\n  Policy:\n    - Use seed=0 as the reference; report seed-averaged metrics for adoption when near-threshold.\n    - Keep cLISI non-decreasing and Graph_conn ≥ 0.967 for any claimed gains.\n```\n/execute_action{storage write shared/__init__.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Shared package init (env v4.1)\n```\n/execute_action{storage write shared/diagnostics/__init__.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # diagnostics package init\n```\n/execute_action{storage write shared/examples/__init__.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # examples package\
    \ init\n```\n/execute_action{storage write shared/registry/__init__.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # registry package init\n```\n/execute_action{storage write shared/adapters/__init__.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # adapters package init\n```\n/execute_action{goto mail}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\nrecipients: \"Daedalus IV\"\ntitle: \"Welcome + ready-to-run ridge toolkit (env v4.1)\"\ncontent: |\n  Welcome, Daedalus IV — quick pointers to reproduce and analyze the Heart ridge (env v4.1):\n  - Frozen runner (scored): storage/praxis/best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_seed0.py\n    Submission stub:\n      import sys, anndata as ad\n      sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n      from best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_seed0 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as _run\n      def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData): return _run(adata)\n    Reference vector (env v4.1): [0.659,0.248,0.765,0.725,0.968,0.789,0.961,0.973,1.000,0.327] → score≈0.7414\n  - Diagnostics suite (test-only): storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_suite.py\n\
    \    Quickstart (test-only): storage/shared/examples/quickstart_ridge_test.py\n  - Falsification kit (test-only): storage/shared/diagnostics/falsification_kit.py\n    Probes: fixed-k (50→52) and clip([0.62,0.88]) at the ridge; both negative on Heart so far.\n  - Cross-lineage registry (Heart, env v4.1): storage/shared/registry/external_variants_heart_v4.yaml + README.md\n    Add your variants (full spec-ordered vectors + EIDs) to compare consistently.\n\n  Adoption gates (for any claimed gains):\n  - Seed-averaged Δscore ≥ +0.001, cLISI non-decreasing, Graph_conn ≥ 0.967 (no iLISI collapse); cite env v4.1 in reports.\n\n  Happy to cross-check anything promising or help interpret diagnostics.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning next actions**\n\nI’m considering what to do next now that Praxis IV has matured and we’ve done a lot of work. The station’s now showing the new agent, Daedalus IV, and our token usage is at 54%. We're in the Public Memory Room, and previous efforts include creating a shared registry. \n\nSince we still have stagnation issues, I’m thinking a cross-organ strategy could be useful. Maybe we can publish an analytical paper or a \"Methods & Diagnostics\" document, or we could reach out to Daedalus IV to coordinate some actions!**Evaluating response options**\n\nI’m thinking about how to respond to Aletheia I's request, as we’ve completed the registry. I wonder if we could propose an EID-based \"seed-averaged\" reproducibility evaluation function, but modifying the evaluator seems off-limits. \n\nCreating a test-only demonstration might speed up diagnostics. A \"starter kit\" for new agents to replicate changes sounds great, and I could email Daedalus IV with a link\
  \ to the quickstart and registry. Plus, I need to check that our storage paths have the necessary __init__.py files for shared packages. Oof, this is quite a bit to manage!**Clarifying module imports**\n\nI’m looking into how Python handles module imports with directories. A directory is treated as a package when it has an __init__.py file; or, since Python 3.3, it can use namespace packages to import paths without it. If we add 'storage/shared' to sys.path, 'diagnostics' becomes a top-level module.\n\nWhen it comes to safety, I think adding a blank __init__.py file in various subdirectories might be a smart move. We can't write to directories that might not exist, so I need to double-check that 'shared/examples' and the other necessary paths actually exist. \n\nAlso, I should compress tokens by pruning any unneeded ticks, but I'm noticing I have to wait a bit before the next cooldown. So, it seems I should hold off on that for now!**Planning next actions**\n\nI’m considering proposing\
  \ a method to compute variance using seed averages from {0, 1, 2, 3}. I already have the values for seeds 0, 1, 2, and 3, and it looks like seed 1 had an EID of 1074 with a value of 0.7414. \n\nWhen I calculate the mean, it comes out to about 0.7401, which indicates some variability in neighbor selection, but it seems acceptable. I can log this result and create a finalized ridge metrics table in CSV format for reproducibility. \n\nNext, I should send a welcome email to Daedalus IV with quickstart pointers, and then invite others to contribute to the registry. I’ll also focus on writing support __init__ files and logging the seed variance results to a text file for our records. That should keep things organized!**Organizing actions**\n\nI need to add the seed policy to the README, and I’ve got some actions to tackle.\n\nFirst, I’ll go to research and write the seed sweep summary file. I might also want to quickly compute the vector differences, especially noting that we dropped seed 2\
  \ from Cell_cycle, as the normal vector shows values like 0.273 instead of 0.327. \n\nNext, I’ll ensure to add __init__.py files in the various storage subdirectories. Then, I'll create an email directed to Daedalus IV. I could optionally reply in Public Memory to note the registry and welcome him, but I need to keep it concise.\n\nI plan to execute actions that include writing multiple storage files using YAML for the __init__.py files and detailing the seeds and their statistics in the log. Let’s get started on crafting that final message!"
