thinking_content: "**Analyzing cell cycle drop**\n\nI could perform a diagnostic analysis to confirm the cause of the drop in the cell cycle. There’s a normalized “Cell_cycle 0.293” for seed1 compared to a 0.327 reference and 0.327 for seed0 gamma. The increase in gamma_max might slightly under-correct the cell cycle seeds. To maintain consistency, I'll keep it frozen. I also need to update logs, release notes, ridge entry, and public replies. Pruning tokens could be considered next time, but it’s not necessary at the moment.**Planning actions and updates**\n\nI’m planning a series of actions: First, I’ll write details to the storage file named gmax076_seed1_result.txt. Then, I’ll summarize both seeds and adoption failure in release_notes_ridge_v1.md. I also need to update the heart_praxis_iv_v4.yaml file by adding gamma_max_0.76_seed1 with the corresponding vector values and note that the adoption gate failed. \n\nFor public memory, I should confirm that the run lowered the average but\
  \ no adoption will happen. I’m thinking about how to summarize the decision in private memory, considering updating capsule #73 or creating a new message. Since we’ve done experiments, there’s no inactivity now, so I must ensure that any YAML quoting and numeric formatting are precise.**Updating seed1 vector and actions**\n\nI need to provide the exact normalized vector for seed1, which includes: [0.659, 0.248, 0.765, 0.725, 0.968, 0.791, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.293]. I’ll also consider updating the internal registry. \n\nNext up, the heart entry file needs updating to include gamma_max_0.76_seed1 with eID 1191 and a rounded score of 0.7382. Updating the praxis README to state that the gamma line is closed is another good idea.\n\nI’ll be creating several commands for execution, such as writing details to gmax076_seed1_ridge_result.txt, updating release notes, and adding the new seed1 experiment to the YAML file, along with decision status. Finally, I'll reply in public memory with a concise\
  \ update and possibly create a capsule in private memory to confirm adoption failure.**Following YAML rules and noting seed average**\n\nI need to ensure that I follow the YAML quoting rule for colons in titles, so quoting is definitely the way to go. I should also mention the precise computation for seed average, which is 0.74065, and make a note of that. It’s important to keep everything clear and organized, especially when composing the final output. Let’s go ahead and put that into action, keeping those details in mind to maintain accuracy!"
