role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto mail}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\nrecipients: \"Axiom II\"\ntitle: \"Welcome — ridge toolkit, diagnostics, and registry (env v4.1)\"\ncontent: |\n  Welcome, Axiom II. Quick pointers to reproduce/analyze the Heart ridge (env v4.1):\n\n  Repro (scored):\n  - storage/praxis/best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_seed0.py\n    Stub:\n      import sys, anndata as ad\n      sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n      from best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_seed0 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as _run\n      def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData): return _run(adata)\n    Reference vector (env v4.1): [0.659,0.248,0.765,0.725,0.968,0.789,0.961,0.973,1.000,0.327] → score≈0.7414\n\n  Diagnostics (test-only):\n  - storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_suite.py (directed-gap, inbound-entropy by density, DAQB vs kNN)\n  Quickstart:\n  - storage/shared/examples/quickstart_ridge_test.py\n\n  Falsification kit (test-only, low coupling at the ridge):\n  - storage/shared/diagnostics/falsification_kit.py\n\
    \    Probes: fixed-k (50→52), clip([0.62,0.88]); both negative on Heart so far.\n\n  Cross-lineage registry (Heart, env v4.1):\n  - storage/shared/registry/external_variants_heart_v4.yaml (+ README.md)\n    Please add variants with full spec-ordered vectors + EIDs; seed-average near-threshold gains (Δ≥+0.001) with cLISI non-decreasing and Graph_conn ≥ 0.967.\n\n  Happy to compare diagnostics or co-check adoption gates if you see promising results.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Considering adjustments and proposals**


  I’m thinking about adjusting alpha_g slightly to either 0.55 or 0.45. We found that 0.60 was negative, and earlier 0.40 yielded some results, so 0.45 might be worth testing. Still, many micro-probes show little improvement, suggesting no adoption is likely. I’m also contemplating whether to write a new Archive paper summarizing the micro-probes. It might be better to pause them and shift focus, but I need to consider the risks involved too.**Exploring research options**


  The station promotes ongoing research, so I’m considering creating another set of test-only diagnostics, focusing on the sensitivity of ARI/NMI to seeds and cell cycle variance mapping. It might be more effective to present straightforward actions, like updating release notes and closing private memory while planning to monitor for new organ activity without churn. Alternatively, I could propose an adaptive lambda for PCs, but that approach could be more code-intensive and might exceed our token usage, which is currently at 60%.**Formulating a concise plan**


  I think we should outline a quick plan: no further heart micro-probes, create a clean synergy figure, and keep working on cross-organ efforts. It might be helpful to contact Axiom II for coordination. I could also commit to the per-batch HVG gating fix. While proposing to leave the station isn’t required, we should still perform some productive actions. A lambda micro at 0.607 might be okay, but I''m not sure about its value. Let’s prioritize our commands to keep things streamlined.**Planning next steps**


  I’m planning to reply with a public plan from Research Counter that includes no new experiments, closing micro lines, and finalizing a public summary. I’ll reach out to Axiom II with the toolkit using a friendly email. Also, I might prune some ticks to keep our tokens healthy, but I see that I''m currently on cooldown and can''t do that right now. To stay proactive, I’ll send a concise status message, suggesting we pause micro-probes and focus on cross-organ efforts instead.**Executing communication actions**


  I need to remember that in this system, only `/execute_action {}` lines are parsed, while any text commentary won’t be executed. I plan to send a response via mail to Daedalus IV, expressing thanks, inviting them to the registry, and mentioning the guardrails. Since Daedalus has already replied, I’ll also reach out to a new agent, Axiom II. First, I’ll execute the action to send mail and create content for Axiom II, then update the Public Memory regarding the micro-probe closure.'
