tick: 859
role: model
parts:
- text: "Reflection Tick 2/3 — Detail Ideas 2 and 3, minimal sweeps, diagnostics, and guardrails\n\nIdea 2: Local distance z-scoring (re-rank per-row)\n- Mechanism (baseline-preserving):\n  - Build a k′-NN candidate list in PCA(60) with cosine (k′ > k to allow re-ranking slack).\n  - For each row i: z-score its candidate distances di (subtract mean μi and divide by std σi+ε), then select the top-k by smallest zi. Keep binary edges; store true distances (min of directed) on the symmetrized adjacency.\n- Rationale:\n  - Removes per-cell scale heterogeneity that favors dense within-batch cores; compares neighbors by relative, not absolute, separation.\n- Minimal hyper-sweep:\n  - Candidate pool size k′ ∈ {k+10, k+20}.\n  - Standardizer: mean/std (z) vs robust median/MAD (zrobust).\n  - Optional winsor on zi at q ∈ {0.99} to avoid extreme leverage.\n- Guardrails (vs baseline PCA+kNN):\n  - Accept if Δscore ≥ +0.003 with cLISI non-decreasing and Graph_conn drop ≤ 0.003.\n  - Secondary: iLISI↑\
    \ and ASW_label not worse by >0.005.\n- Diagnostics to print:\n  - Per-row μi, σi summary (median/IQR); fraction of rows with σi near 0 (degenerate).\n  - Shift in cross-batch fraction (median/IQR) vs baseline; Jaccard (median) vs baseline graph.\n  - Degree distribution (should equal k after sym), connected components (expect 1), Graph_conn.\n- Edge cases/mitigations:\n  - Small σi (nearly identical distances): add ε=1e-8 and fall back to raw distance ordering when σi < σmin (e.g., 1e-6).\n  - Candidate pool size too small: if ties or empties, increase to k+20 fallback per-row.\n\nIdea 3: Mutual‑k enforcement with distance-only top‑up\n- Mechanism:\n  - Build directed k‑NN A.\n  - Keep mutual edges M = min(A, Aᵀ) (binary). This reduces unilateral/hub edges without any batch information.\n  - Top‑up to degree k using distance-only criteria: candidates = union of out- and in-neighbors; sort by true distance; add until degree≈k; then symmetrize by max to preserve undirectedness.\n- Variants\
    \ (very light):\n  - Soft‑mutual: accept edges where j∈NNk(i) and i∈NNr(i) with r ∈ {k, k+10}, before top‑up. This increases mutually agreeable edges without enforcing batch structure.\n  - Minimal mutual fraction: require per-row fmut ≥ fmin ∈ {0.2, 0.5} before top‑up (still distance-only, no batch signal).\n- Expected effects:\n  - iLISI/kBET: may improve (fewer unilateral within-batch links).\n  - Graph_conn: risk of underfilling/fragmentation if mutual core is too small—top‑up must restore degrees.\n  - ASW_label: neutral to mildly negative; monitor for label over-smoothing.\n- Guardrails (vs baseline PCA+kNN):\n  - Accept if Δscore ≥ +0.003 with Graph_conn ≥ baseline−0.003 and cLISI non-decreasing.\n  - If Graph_conn falls below 0.95, reject regardless of score (practical usability).\n- Diagnostics:\n  - Reciprocity distribution (per-row fraction of mutual edges before top‑up).\n  - Degree histogram after top‑up (min/mean/95%ile) and connected components count.\n  - Jaccard vs baseline;\
    \ cbf (cross-batch fraction) distribution shift; summary of top‑up fraction per node (how many edges were mutual vs added).\n\nComplexity/time budget and invariants\n- kNN build dominates (O(n·k′) memory; brute-force is acceptable for ~20k×60 with cosine).\n- Per-row re-ranking/top‑up is O(k′ log k′), easily within limits.\n- Outputs strictly adhere to evaluator contract: binary connectivities; true distances CSR; no batch-based choices; seed fixed.\n\nInterpretation framework (for both ideas)\n- Primary movement targets on the simple baseline: iLISI↑ and kBET↑ with cLISI stable; maintain Graph_conn.\n- If score improves but Graph_conn drops more than 0.003 or cLISI worsens, treat as non-adopt.\n- If both ideas show slight but consistent gains (Δ≈+0.002 to +0.003), prefer the one with lower perturbation (higher Jaccard to baseline) and better cLISI stability.\n\nNext (Tick 3)\n- Synthesis plan: if either Idea 2 or 3 passes baseline gates, define a minimal port to the ridge context (still\
    \ batch-agnostic, distance-only) and test synergy in that stricter setting under the frozen runner constraints. If neither passes, consolidate as informative negatives and consider a fourth orthogonal idea (e.g., neighborhood de-duplication by angular margin or per-axis whitening limited to top-variance PCs) for a quick attempt."
