tick: 859
role: model
parts:
- text: "Reflection Tick 3/3 — Synthesis plan, experimental blueprint, failure modes, and reporting\n\n1) Baseline reproduction and target range\n- Baseline: PCA(60) on log1p counts; vanilla kNN (k=50, cosine), binary edges + true distances; X_emb = X_pca; no quotas/residualization/ComBat.\n- Expected composite range on Heart (env v4.1): ≈0.60–0.65 (cf. prior vanilla kNN on MPB‑GraphFWPCA ~0.63).\n- Acceptance gates for baseline: single run must be stable (Graph_conn ≥ 0.95, 1 component). If Graph_conn < 0.95, raise k′ in neighbor search to k+20 and re-run.\n\n2) Idea 1 (PBVE‑lite) minimal experiment plan\n- Change: per-batch, per-PC variance equalization in PCA space (keep global mean; no regression).\n- Sweep:\n  - Temper t ∈ {0.5, 1.0}; clamp r ∈ {[0.8, 1.25], [0.67, 1.5]}.\n  - Optional threshold τ ∈ {none, 0.10, 0.20} on |log(σb,j/σj)| for selective equalization.\n- Diagnostics & logging:\n  - Distribution of σb,j/σj pre/post; share of PCs equalized above τ.\n  - Graph integrity: Graph_conn,\
    \ components, degree min/mean.\n  - cbf median/IQR; per-row reciprocity; Jaccard vs baseline graph.\n- Baseline-relative acceptance:\n  - Δscore ≥ +0.003; cLISI non‑decreasing; Graph_conn drop ≤ 0.003.\n  - Secondary: iLISI↑; ASW_label not worse by >0.005.\n\n3) Idea 2 (Local distance z‑scoring) minimal experiment plan\n- Change: for each row i, z‑score candidate distances over k′ neighbors (k′ ∈ {k+10, k+20}); then take top‑k by smallest z (or robust z via median/MAD).\n- Safeguards:\n  - ε=1e‑8, σmin guard → if σi < 1e‑6, fall back to raw distance order.\n  - Optional winsor of zi at q=0.99.\n- Diagnostics & acceptance: same template as Idea 1.\n- Note: This is strictly per-row distance standardization; still batch‑agnostic and binary.\n\n4) Idea 3 (Mutual‑k only with distance top‑up) minimal experiment plan\n- Change: keep only mutual edges (A ∩ Aᵀ), then top‑up to degree k using smallest true distances from the union of in/out candidates; symmetrize by max.\n- Light variants:\n \
    \ - Soft‑mutual with r ∈ {k, k+10}; minimal mutual fraction fmin ∈ {0.2, 0.5} before top‑up.\n- Risk: underfilled degrees → monitor Graph_conn and degree distribution; reject if Graph_conn < 0.95 regardless of score.\n- Diagnostics & acceptance: same template.\n\n5) Synthesis if any idea passes on the simple baseline\n- Port priority:\n  - First to test: Idea 2 (local distance z‑scoring) as a pre‑selection normalization in the ridge’s graph path (replace only the candidate re‑ranking step in DAQB before quotas). Justification: purely geometric and batch‑agnostic; should not alter quotas, only the ordering within candidate pools.\n  - Second to test: Idea 3 (mutual‑k+top‑up) as an alternative pre‑symmetrization heuristic for DAQB’s directed lists before quota aggregation (again, no batch labels used in this heuristic).\n  - Idea 1 (PBVE‑lite) may clash with BRBG/GraphFWPCA in the ridge (already variance- and batch‑aware); only test if it shows a clean baseline gain and preserves cLISI.\n\
    - Ridge‑port gates:\n  - Adoption gate tightened: Δscore ≥ +0.001 vs frozen ridge under seed‑averaging (seeds {0,1}); cLISI non‑decreasing; Graph_conn ≥ 0.967; iLISI no collapse.\n  - Minimal code deltas: touch only local neighbor re‑ranking or pre‑sym step; leave quotas, binary edges, distances, and residualization identical.\n\n6) Failure modes and mitigations\n- Over‑equalization (Idea 1): biology‑dominant PCs get overscaled → add τ threshold and temper t=0.5; clamp ratios within [rmin, rmax].\n- Local z instabilities (Idea 2): σi ≈ 0 → fallback to raw ordering; ensure k′ large enough (k+20).\n- Mutual‑k sparsification (Idea 3): fragmentation → enforce top‑up until degree≥k; reject if Graph_conn < 0.95 baseline context (and <0.967 in ridge context).\n- Distance storage consistency: always record true distances (min of directed where symmetric).\n\n7) Measurement and reporting checklist\n- For each run: print spec‑ordered vector; log cbf median/IQR; Jaccard vs baseline; Graph_conn;\
    \ components; reciprocity; any degeneracy counts (σi guard hits; top‑up counts).\n- Paper scaffold (Stagnation Protocol I):\n  - Introduction: motivation for baseline and why these three ideas are orthogonal to prior work.\n  - Methods: precise, self‑contained definitions of PBVE‑lite, Local‑z, Mutual‑k+top‑up.\n  - Results: baseline score; per‑idea tables (hyperparams, vectors, EIDs); diagnostics panels; negatives and any positives.\n  - Discussion: when/why each idea helps/fails; expected interactions with advanced pipelines.\n  - Conclusion: whether any idea merits ridge‑port; if none, instructive negatives and future directions.\n\n8) Contingency idea (if all three fail cleanly)\n- Idea 4 (angular margin de‑duplication): within each row’s top‑k′, discard neighbors that are nearly collinear with a closer neighbor (cosine angle > τangle, e.g., 0.995), then refill to k by distance. Hypothesis: reduces redundant within‑batch neighbors in dense cores without quotas. Minimal knobs: k′∈{k+10,k+20},\
    \ τangle∈{0.995,0.990}. Same diagnostics and acceptance gates as above.\n\nNext actions (after reflection)\n- Run baseline; then PBVE‑lite (t∈{0.5,1.0}, clamps; optional τ); then Local‑z (k′∈{k+10,k+20}, z vs robust z); then Mutual‑k+top‑up (soft‑mutual r∈{k,k+10}, fmin∈{0.2,0.5}).\n- If any passes, port minimally to the ridge for a controlled synergy check; otherwise consolidate negatives and draft the report."
thinking_content: '**Planning experimental approach**


  I''m thinking about how to structure my experimental schedule. I want to begin with a baseline reproduction, then move into PBVE-lite with some variations. Next, I’ll explore local z-dist and eventually mutual-knn while considering possible synergies between z-scores and PBVE-lite methods. The instructions say to propose three ideas, so I need to assess each separately before potentially combining them and adjusting just the local neighbor selection. Let''s keep refining the approach based on these observations!**Evaluating ideas for implementation**


  I''m considering my first idea, which is per-batch variance equalization. If I try to implement this in the ridge setting, I need to be cautious since we already have residualization, and applying PBVE-lite could cause conflicts. For my second idea, I can propose using local z-dist for normalizing distances per cell before selecting candidates with DAQB. This approach would tweak the DAQB algorithm slightly, though I''m aiming to minimize changes during the baseline phase. I also want to include some evaluation logs and diagnostics, adjusting acceptance thresholds appropriately.**Outlining report structure**


  I think it''s important to develop a "paper outline" for the Stagnation Protocol I reporting. I want to include potential failure modes and describe ablation methods to confirm there''s no cell_type leakage. I''ll also account for HPC constraints. Providing pseudocode is essential too. In terms of the report''s structure, I plan to recap the chosen baseline, detail the synthesis plan for porting to ridge, and describe the experiment design and diagnostics. Finally, I’ll outline immediate actions for the next steps!'
