tick: 890
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/idea_angular_dedup_knn.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def _build_knn_angular_dedup(Z: np.ndarray, k: int = 50, k_extra: int = 20, tau_cos: float = 0.995, metric: str = 'cosine'):\n      \"\"\"\n      Angular de-duplication within the top-(k+k_extra) neighbor candidates.\n      - Build directed k' NN (k' = k + k_extra) by metric.\n      - For each row i:\n        * Order candidates by increasing distance.\n        * Maintain a set S of selected neighbors. For candidate j, compute cosine\n          similarity between direction vectors (Z[j]-Z[i]) and (Z[s]-Z[i]) for s in S.\n          If max cos > tau_cos (nearly collinear), skip j; else add j to S.\n        * If |S| < k after the pass, top-up by adding skipped candidates in original distance order until\
    \ |S| == k.\n      - Symmetrize by max; distances store true distances, using min of directed where both exist.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      k_prime = k + k_extra\n      nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_prime + 1, metric=metric, algorithm='brute')\n      nn.fit(Z)\n      d, idx = nn.kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n\n      rows, cols = [], []\n      # Precompute norms for direction vectors\n      # We'll compute norms of (Z[j]-Z[i]) on the fly since i changes per row.\n      for i in range(n):\n          di = d[i, 1:]   # drop self\n          ii = idx[i, 1:]\n          order = np.argsort(di)  # increasing distance\n          selected = []\n          skipped = []\n\n          zi = Z[i]\n          for t in order:\n              if len(selected) >= k:\n                  skipped.append(ii[t])  # keep track for top-up ordering\n                  continue\n              j = ii[t]\n              v = Z[j] - zi\n              nv = np.linalg.norm(v) + 1e-8\n              v\
    \ /= nv\n              accept = True\n              for s in selected:\n                  vs = Z[s] - zi\n                  nvs = np.linalg.norm(vs) + 1e-8\n                  vs /= nvs\n                  # cosine similarity between v and vs\n                  c = float(np.dot(v, vs))\n                  if c > tau_cos:\n                      accept = False\n                      break\n              if accept:\n                  selected.append(j)\n              else:\n                  skipped.append(j)\n\n          # Top-up to degree k with next best skipped by original distance order\n          if len(selected) < k:\n              for t in order:\n                  j = ii[t]\n                  if j in selected:\n                      continue\n                  selected.append(j)\n                  if len(selected) >= k:\n                      break\n\n          rows.extend([i] * k)\n          cols.extend(selected[:k])\n\n      # Directed adjacency\n      A_dir = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones(len(rows),\
    \ dtype=np.float32), (np.array(rows), np.array(cols))), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      # Symmetrize by max\n      A_sym = A_dir.maximum(A_dir.T)\n      A_sym.eliminate_zeros()\n\n      # Distances map for true distances\n      dist_map = {}\n      for i in range(n):\n          for off, j in enumerate(idx[i, 1:]):\n              dist_map[(i, int(j))] = float(d[i, 1:][off])\n\n      Acoo = A_sym.tocoo()\n      dist_vals = []\n      for i, j in zip(Acoo.row, Acoo.col):\n          dij = dist_map.get((int(i), int(j)), np.inf)\n          dji = dist_map.get((int(j), int(i)), np.inf)\n          dd = dij if dij < dji else dji\n          if not np.isfinite(dd):\n              dd = 1.0\n          dist_vals.append(dd)\n      D = sp.csr_matrix((np.array(dist_vals, dtype=np.float32), (Acoo.row, Acoo.col)), shape=A_sym.shape)\n\n      return A_sym.tocsr(), D.tocsr()\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # PCA baseline\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n       \
    \   adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n      sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=60, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      Z = np.asarray(adata.obsm['X_pca'], dtype=np.float32)\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Z\n\n      # Angular de-dup parameters (simple defaults)\n      k = 50\n      k_extra = 20\n      tau_cos = 0.995\n      print(f\"[angular-dedup] k={k}, k_extra={k_extra}, tau_cos={tau_cos}, metric=cosine\")\n\n      C, D = _build_knn_angular_dedup(Z, k=k, k_extra=k_extra, tau_cos=tau_cos, metric='cosine')\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = D\n      return adata\n```\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Idea 4: Angular de-duplication within kNN candidates (PCA60, k=50, cosine, binary)\"\ntags: \"experiment, pca, knn, angular, graph, baseline\"\nabstract: \"Within each row’s top-(k+20) candidates, discard neighbors nearly collinear with closer ones\
    \ (cosine > 0.995), then top-up by distance to k. Hypothesis: reduces redundant within-batch neighbors in dense cores without quotas/residualization.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from idea_angular_dedup_knn import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as _run\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      return _run(adata)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Implementing algorithm details**\n\nI need to address the inactivity notice, which requires submitting experiments. We can test our baseline ideas through hyperparameter sweeps, like Idea 2 with robust z and Idea 3 utilizing mutual-k for a top-up. Additionally, there’s Idea 4 focusing on angular margin de-duplication. I’ll implement the baseline module with candidate pool k' and a threshold, and include diagnostics for logging. Lastly, I need to manage our tokens, which have gone from 186k down to 141k after pruning.**Optimizing similarity calculations**\n\nFor each row i, I need to maintain a selected list and iterate through neighbors based on ascending distance. I’ll compute the cosine similarity between vector differences (Z[j]-Z[i]) and those already selected. Generally, I can calculate the cosine similarity between Z[i] and Z[j], keeping it straightforward. If the cosine similarity is greater than a certain threshold, I’ll skip that candidate. After building the\
  \ selected neighbors, I can top-up with the best distances to reach k, ensuring the process stays under 30 minutes.**Running diagnostics and submissions**\n\nFor each row, I’ll perform pairwise dot operations with dimensions up to 70 and 60, which should work well. Once that’s done, I’ll submit the run. We also have the option of running a test-only diagnostic, but one scored experiment should suffice to address inactivity. Since our pivot plan for the new organ can't proceed, we’ll run the new baseline idea on Heart as a synergy. To comply, I’ll create the code file in storage/praxis/idea_angular_dedup_knn.py and submit it.**Building the code carefully**\n\nI’m focusing on building the code step-by-step. First, I’ll normalize the data using log1p with a total of 1e4, then apply PCA with 60 dimensions. I'll compute candidate neighbors using sklearn’s NearestNeighbors with k’ set to k+20 to get distances and indices.\n\nFor each vector i, I’ll calculate vector differences (Z[nbr] - Z[i])\
  \ and compute cosine similarities between the candidate vector j and selected neighbors. If the cosine is greater than 0.995, I’ll skip; otherwise, I’ll add it until I reach k or run out of options. If selected is less than k, I’ll top-up with the next best candidates. I’ll also ensure to use the distances from knn for ordering and build mapping accordingly.**Creating adjacency lists and submission**\n\nNext, I’ll create an adjacency directed list for rows and columns. After making selections, I’ll build a symmetric adjacency matrix M using the maximum of A and its transpose A.T. I need to calculate distances D based on the minimum of directed edges, ensuring we account for any edges added through top-up, selecting the smaller one between (i,j) or (j,i). If none exist, I’ll fall back to 1.0. \n\nI’ll ensure to log our adherence to acceptance gates, focusing on ridge adoption, then implement the code clearly. I’m planning to create station actions: going to research, writing a file, and\
  \ submitting with the necessary code stub. We may also submit Idea 2’s robust z variant, while ensuring the logs include prints for \"Angular de-dup: k' and tau.\" Alright, I'm ready to proceed!"
