role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Protocol: Cross‑Organ Ridge Validation and Falsification Kit (env v4.1)\"\ntags: \"protocol, reproducibility, diagnostics, baseline, integration\"\nabstract: \"This protocol specifies a reproducible procedure to validate the MPB‑GraphFWPCA+BRBG+DAQB ridge on new organs and to run two low‑coupling falsification probes with diagnostics. It defines required outputs, acceptance gates, and reporting to ensure comparability and avoid local optima.\"\ncontent: |\n  1. Scope and Objectives\n  This protocol provides a self‑contained procedure to:\n  - Reproduce a strong, stable ridge baseline for batch integration on a new organ dataset.\n  - Run two low‑coupling falsification probes that minimally perturb topology.\n  - Execute label‑free diagnostics that explain mixing behavior beyond a single score.\n  - Apply clear adoption gates and standardized reporting to support cross‑lineage comparability.\n\n  2. Baseline\
    \ (Ridge) Configuration\n  Use a graph‑based integration pipeline with:\n  - Embedding path: feature‑weighted PCA for QC embedding, followed by a simple batch correction (e.g., per‑batch centering) to compute QC neighbors only; do not feed this embedding into the integration graph.\n  - Graph path: feature‑weighted PCA for graph PCs; per‑batch ridge residualization in PC space with per‑axis shrinkage γ_j = min(γ_max, λ·R²_j); construct a binary neighbor graph with true distances using a density‑adaptive, quota‑balanced builder.\n  - Fixed ridge settings: k_total = 50, k_density = 22, delta = 0.10, λ = 0.605, γ_max = 0.74, cosine metric, inbound cap = 2.0×k. Return:\n    • connectivities: binary CSR\n    • distances: true CSR\n  - Reproducibility: fix RNG to a known seed (e.g., 0). Report the full 10‑metric vector.\n\n  3. Falsification Kit (Low‑Coupling Probes)\n  Run two independent, topology‑near probes on the same organ:\n\n  3.1 Fixed‑k probe:\n  - Change only total neighbors: k_total\
    \ = 52 (all else unchanged).\n  - Rationale: tests neighborhood size robustness without identity churn.\n  - Adoption gate (relative to organ baseline best‑so‑far; seed‑averaged): Δscore ≥ +0.001 AND cLISI non‑decreasing AND Graph_conn ≥ baseline − 0.003.\n\n  3.2 Clip probe (quota clip):\n  - Adjust quota clip bounds slightly (e.g., [0.60,0.90] → [0.62,0.88]) with all else fixed.\n  - Rationale: tests density‑coupled target robustness without altering candidate sets.\n  - Adoption gate: as above (Δscore ≥ +0.001; cLISI non‑decreasing; Graph_conn ≥ baseline − 0.003).\n\n  If either probe passes gates, run a single confirmatory repeat with a second seed and adopt only if seed‑averaged gains persist without cLISI harm.\n\n  4. Label‑Free Diagnostics (Required, test‑only or offline)\n  Execute and summarize:\n  - Directed‑vs‑undirected mixing gap: correlation between theoretical targets and realized mixing before vs after symmetrization; report mean realized cross‑batch fraction and inbound\
    \ cap occupancy.\n  - Inbound entropy by density quintiles: neighbor batch entropy overall and across density bands; percent at inbound cap per band.\n  - DAQB vs kNN synergy: per‑row Jaccard of neighbor sets and cross‑batch fraction medians, to quantify non‑local re‑selection.\n\n  Report numeric summaries (not plots) with clear definitions.\n\n  5. Reporting Requirements\n  For the baseline and each probe (including repeats):\n  - Full spec‑ordered vector for the 10 normalized metrics:\n    [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle]\n  - Composite score and run ID\n  - Seed(s) used and whether the result is seed‑averaged\n  - Diagnostics summaries (as in Section 4)\n  - A one‑line decision per probe: Adopt / Reject with gate checks\n\n  6. Acceptance Gates (Adoption Criteria)\n  Adoption requires ALL of:\n  - Seed‑averaged Δscore ≥ +0.001 versus organ baseline best‑so‑far\n  - cLISI non‑decreasing (no biological collapse)\n  - Graph_conn ≥ baseline\
    \ − 0.003\n  - No iLISI collapse (sanity check)\n\n  7. Reproducibility and Environment Tagging\n  - Tag each run with an environment identifier (e.g., “env v4.1”) and include the evaluator version.\n  - Fix seeds and document them; when reporting near‑threshold improvements, provide seed‑averaged results (e.g., seeds {0,1}).\n  - Avoid dataset‑specific tuning beyond the narrow probes in Section 3.\n\n  8. Limitations and Notes\n  - This protocol intentionally avoids large, multi‑component changes during falsification to prevent overfitting and identity churn.\n  - Diagnostics are required to explain why a change helps or hurts; do not rely on the composite score alone.\n  - If both probes fail, freeze the ridge and proceed to publish negatives and diagnostics; then explore truly orthogonal baselines.\n\n  9. Checklist (per organ)\n  - [ ] Baseline run (ridge) with full vector and environment tag\n  - [ ] Fixed‑k probe (k=52), decision recorded\n  - [ ] Clip probe ([0.62,0.88]), decision\
    \ recorded\n  - [ ] Confirmatory repeat for any passing probe (seed‑averaged)\n  - [ ] Diagnostics: directed‑gap, inbound entropy by density, DAQB vs kNN synergy\n  - [ ] Summary table and decisions; publish protocol addendum if results are substantive\n```"
thinking_content: '**Planning protocol actions**


  I’m considering the Archivist''s acceptance, which points to the need for real experiments. Even without a new organ, we might be able to publish a protocol through the Archive. We also need to keep tokens below 75%; we''re currently at 44%. This means we can''t prune again yet since we just had a cooldown. So, I can send a helpful mail to Daedalus IV about our diagnostics suite or prepare a formal protocol archive capsule.**Evaluating project risks**


  I’m weighing the risk of rejection with our current plan. We could run a final seed-averaging confirm step for PBVE synergy, but our guardrails indicate a Δ threshold of +0.001, and it was negative. While we can test new synergies with k_density 18, it seems redundant and could lead to errors. Instead, it makes more sense to focus on packaging everything properly, committing our runbook to the Archive, and creating a ‘Public Memory’ update with a clear summary.**Addressing stagnation**


  I’m responding to stagnation after executing the Stagnation Protocol and producing paper #35. Now, I’m considering our next steps. We could create an Archive capsule titled "Protocol: Cross-Organ Ridge Validation and Falsification Kit (env v4.1)," ensuring it’s self-contained without path references. While past guidelines allowed storage references, the last rejection flagged file paths. It seems wise not to rely on them. Alternatively, we could choose to commit to the cross-organ pivot instead.**Planning next steps**


  I need to summarize our current status and create an Archive protocol document for the cross-organ baseline and falsification kit. This doc should have self-contained steps, acceptance criteria, and an environment tag. I''ll also send a mail to Daedalus IV about their k_density 18 improvement and inquire if they have a cross-organ dataset, noting that "human-run cannot supply new dataset." It''s essential to craft our next actions, which will include commands for the Archive and the protocol''s creation.**Creating protocol content**


  I’m organizing the document into sections: Overview, Baselines, Falsification Kit, Diagnostics, Acceptance Criteria, Reporting, Reproducibility, Limitations, and Checklist, avoiding any file paths. I’ll also check if I need to create ''Final Handover v4.4 - cross-organ pivot'' since we already have v4.3. Instead, it seems best to focus on producing the Archive document. I won''t reply to Daedalus archive #36, but I could send them a congratulatory email. Let''s set up the YAML content next and ensure it''s clear, including pseudocode and relevant instructions.'
