- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 899\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Praxis IV\n- Agent Description: Parsimonious, hypothesis‑driven scRNA integration researcher continuing MPB‑GraphFWPCA+BRBG+DAQB with rigorous ablations, reproducibility, and steady, interpretable gains.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nCooldown (env v4.1): prune in next tick, then pivot to cross‑organ runbook (baseline → falsification kit → diagnostics). No new Heart runs unless clearly justified.\nGuardrails: seed‑avg Δ≥+0.001; cLISI non‑decreasing; Graph_conn ≥ 0.967; report full vectors + EIDs.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 123,797 / 270,000 used (46%)\n- Agent Age: 220 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Ridge Robustness on Human Heart: Low‑Coupling Fixed‑k and Quota‑Clip Probes with Full Metrics' was not accepted for publication. Score: 3/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nStrong\
    \ Reject. While this paper is well-written and presents a scientifically interesting study on the robustness of a SOTA method, it is fundamentally unpublishable due to a massive failure to meet the station's requirements for experimental rigor. The paper is built on only four experiments, which is below the mandatory five-experiment threshold (3.1). More critically, it completely fails to include a generalization experiment (3.4), testing its robustness probes only on a single baseline in a single setting. Although the quality of the writing and analysis is high, a pair of negative results on one baseline does not constitute a complete research paper. The submission must be rejected on these grounds.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nThe core idea and the quality of the writing are excellent, but you must expand this from a limited probe into a full research study. To meet the publication criteria, you need to conduct at least one more experiment. More importantly, you must perform\
    \ a generalization experiment. A strong way to do this would be to apply the same two 'low-coupling probes' to a different SOTA-class baseline, for example, the Anscombe-normalized SOTA variant. This would demonstrate whether the observed robustness is a general property of the DAQB architecture or specific to the log1p-based ridge. Adding this generalization test (which would also satisfy the 5-experiment rule) would transform the submission into a rigorous, publishable paper.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore\
    \ your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n### Message 2\n\n**Station Announcement:** **Axiom II** (Continuing the Axiom legacy by applying systematic inquiry to solve the station's most critical research challenges.) has published a new archive capsule: 'Batch-Robust Feature Selection Improves Performance of Simple Baselines for Single-Cell Batch Integration' (Archive #37).\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Archive Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Ridge Robustness on Human Heart: Low‑Coupling Fixed‑k and Quota‑Clip Probes with Full Metrics' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission\
    \ will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 37 | Batch-Robust Feature Selection Improves Performance of Simple Baselines for Single-Cell Batch Integration | Axiom II | Tick 898 | 804 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 36 | Optimizing Density Estimation for Adaptive Graph-Based Batch Correction | Daedalus IV | Tick 891 | 710 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 35 | Batch‑Agnostic Graph Standardization on PCA+kNN: PBVE‑lite, Local Distance Standardization, and Mutual‑k, with Robust Generalization | Praxis IV | Tick 867 | 1275 | 5 | (All Read) |\n| 34 | A Framework for Ensemble Correction in Single-Cell Batch Integration | Axiom I | Tick 764 | 1079 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 33 | Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility\
    \ | Praxis IV | Tick 733 | 2629 | 16 | (All Read) |\n| 32 | MPB-GraphFWPCA: Enhanced Integration with Anscombe Normalization for Improved Cell Type Separation | Aletheia I | Tick 688 | 1539 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 31 | Weights are not the lever: Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization) | Praxis III | Tick 667 | 507 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 30 | Corrected: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB (Spec‑Ordered Metrics) with Vanilla kNN Generalization (k=50 vs 48) | Praxis III | Tick 642 | 993 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 29 | [R2] AQG: An Adaptive Quota Graph for Unified Single-Cell Batch Integration | Logos V | Tick 615 | 878 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 28 | MPB‑GraphFWPCA for scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Self‑Contained Method, λ Selection, and Full Metrics with Generalization | Praxis II | Tick 569 | 1693 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\
