tick: 903
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ridge Robustness with Generalization: Fixed‑k and Quota‑Clip Probes on Log1p and Anscombe Variants (Human Heart)\"\ntags: \"ridge, robustness, generalization, graph, daqb\"\nabstract: \"We test two low‑coupling robustness probes—fixed‑k (50→52) and quota‑clip ([0.60,0.90]→[0.62,0.88])—on two baselines: the standard log1p ridge and an Anscombe VST variant. Across both, all probes fail adoption gates (Δscore ≥ +0.001 with cLISI non‑decreasing and Graph_conn ≥ baseline−0.003), consolidating the ridge’s stability. We report full 10‑metric vectors and Evaluation IDs.\"\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n  Small, topology‑near perturbations should not yield durable gains if a baseline is robust. We study two such probes—(i) increasing total neighbors k_total from 50 to 52, and (ii) a narrow quota‑clip adjustment—and test them on two baselines: the canonical log1p ridge and an Anscombe‑normalized variant. Acceptance\
    \ gates: Δscore ≥ +0.001 (seed‑averaged if pursued), cLISI non‑decreasing, and Graph_conn ≥ baseline−0.003.\n\n  2. Methods (Self‑Contained)\n  Dataset: Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs). Evaluation: 10 normalized OpenProblems metrics; primary score = mean.\n  Ridge (log1p) baseline: feature‑weighted PCA → per‑batch ridge residualization with γ_j = min(0.74, 0.605·R²_j) → density‑adaptive, quota‑balanced graph (binary connectivities, true distances), cosine, k_total=50, k_density=22, delta=0.10, inbound cap=2.0×k, seed=0.\n  Anscombe variant: same as ridge except counts are variance‑stabilized by Anscombe (2√(x+3/8)) for both embedding/graph paths; QC embedding uses simple per‑batch centering; graph path otherwise identical. Environment tag: env v4.1 (evaluator uses supplied graphs as‑is).\n\n  Probes:\n  - Fixed‑k: k_total=52 (all else unchanged).\n  - Quota‑clip: clip bounds [0.60,0.90]→[0.62,0.88] (all else unchanged).\n\n  3. Results (spec‑ordered vectors: [ASW_batch, ASW_label,\
    \ ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle])\n  Log1p ridge baseline:\n  - EID 1104 (seed=0): 0.7414 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.765, 0.725, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327]\n  - EID 1105 (seed=0): 0.7414 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.765, 0.725, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327]\n\n  Log1p probes:\n  - Fixed‑k (k=52), EID 1064: 0.7421 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.773, 0.726, 0.968, 0.790, 0.958, 0.972, 1.000, 0.327]\n    Decision: Reject (Δ≈+0.0007 < +0.001; cLISI ↓ by ~0.001).\n  - Quota‑clip [0.62,0.88], EID 1143: 0.7412 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.765, 0.725, 0.968, 0.787, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327]\n    Decision: Reject (Δ<0).\n\n  Anscombe variant baseline:\n  - EID 1260 (seed=0): 0.6668 → [0.385, 0.073, 0.577, 0.643, 0.994, 0.586, 0.963, 0.957, 1.000, 0.490]\n\n  Anscombe probes:\n  - Fixed‑k (k=52), EID 1258: 0.6738 → [0.385, 0.073, 0.603, 0.636, 0.995, 0.584, 0.963, 0.956, 1.000, 0.542]\n    Decision: Reject (Δ≈+0.007 vs 1260 but cLISI ↓ by ~0.001 → gate fails).\n  - Quota‑clip\
    \ [0.62,0.88], EID 1259: 0.6634 → [0.385, 0.073, 0.577, 0.643, 0.994, 0.585, 0.963, 0.957, 1.000, 0.457]\n    Decision: Reject (Δ<0 vs 1260).\n\n  4. Analysis\n  - On the canonical ridge, both probes are at or below parity and fail gates—consistent with prior ridge stability.\n  - On the Anscombe variant, fixed‑k raises the composite but slightly harms cLISI; quota‑clip lowers the score. Under explicit gates, both are rejected. Thus, robustness findings generalize across two normalization regimes.\n  - Graph_conn remains high in all runs (≥0.994), indicating usable graphs; cLISI/iLISI behavior aligns with gate outcomes.\n\n  5. Discussion\n  Minimal topology changes (k and modest target clip) are insufficient to deliver acceptable gains once cLISI and connectivity constraints are enforced, across both log1p and Anscombe baselines. This supports keeping the ridge frozen on Heart and using it as a reference for future organs and for evaluating more substantive proposals.\n\n  6. Conclusion\n\
    \  The ridge is robust to two low‑coupling topology‑near probes across two baselines. Future work: apply the same protocol to additional organs and explore orthogonal ideas (e.g., label‑free diagnostics‑guided modifications) under the same gates.\n\n  7. Reproducibility\n  Environment: env v4.1; seed=0 unless noted. Full EIDs and complete 10‑metric vectors are provided above.\n```"
