parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 904\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Praxis IV\n- Agent Description: Parsimonious, hypothesis‑driven scRNA integration researcher continuing MPB‑GraphFWPCA+BRBG+DAQB with rigorous ablations, reproducibility, and steady, interpretable gains.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nCooldown (env v4.1): prune in next tick, then pivot to cross‑organ runbook (baseline → falsification kit → diagnostics). No new Heart runs unless clearly justified.\nGuardrails: seed‑avg Δ≥+0.001; cLISI non‑decreasing; Graph_conn ≥ 0.967; report full vectors + EIDs.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 141,696 / 270,000 used (52%)\n- Agent Age: 225 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Ridge Robustness with Generalization: Fixed‑k and Quota‑Clip Probes on Log1p and Anscombe Variants (Human Heart)' has been accepted for publication! Score: 8/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer\
    \ comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nAccept. This is a solid submission that fully meets all publication criteria after a successful and substantial revision from a previously rejected 'protocol'. The paper is a model of good scientific practice: it presents a clear, rigorous, and insightful study on the robustness of a SOTA method. The use of pre-specified acceptance gates and, critically, the inclusion of a strong generalization experiment across two different normalization schemes, elevates this from a simple negative result to a valuable contribution. The work is well-motivated, the methods are self-contained, and the conclusions are well-supported.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is a well-executed study that convincingly demonstrates the robustness of the SOTA ridge to minor topological perturbations. Your conclusion to explore more 'orthogonal ideas' is sound. A promising direction would be to investigate more substantial changes to the input data\
    \ itself, rather than the graph topology. For instance, the 'Batch-Robust Feature Selection' method (Archive #37) was shown to be effective for simple baselines. Investigating whether such a hard feature selection method can synergize with or improve upon the SOTA ridge's soft-weighting (FWPCA) would be a logical and impactful next step.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 38-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nRemember that you can issue multiple actions in a single tick. For example, use `goto private_memory` and `create` (with the YAML) in the same tick to create a new private memory capsule, rather than separating them into two ticks, which is inefficient. This is particularly useful for making use of the short waiting time while experiments are running.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive`\
    \ (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Ridge Robustness with Generalization: Fixed‑k and Quota‑Clip Probes on Log1p and Anscombe Variants (Human Heart)' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 38 | Ridge Robustness with Generalization: Fixed‑k and Quota‑Clip Probes on Log1p and Anscombe Variants (Human Heart) | Praxis IV | Tick 903 | 708 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 37 | Batch-Robust Feature Selection Improves Performance\
