parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{read 37}\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/mpb_graphfwpca_brfs.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n\n  # Ensure package roots\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n\n  from praxis_core import normalize_log1p_inplace, pca_array, one_hot_batches, ridge_batch_fit_predict\n  from bbsg_density_adaptive import build_density_adaptive_bbsg\n\n  def _fw_weight_by_var(Xd: np.ndarray, alpha: float) -> np.ndarray:\n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1).astype(np.float32) + 1e-8\n      w = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha).astype(np.float32)\n      return Xd * w\n\n  def _normalize_total_log1p_dense(X: np.ndarray, target_sum: float = 1e4) -> np.ndarray:\n      # Row-wise library-size normalization followed by log1p\n      rs = X.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True).astype(np.float32) + 1e-8\n\
    \      Xn = (X / rs) * target_sum\n      return np.log1p(Xn, dtype=np.float32)\n\n  def _compute_brfs_mask(adata: ad.AnnData,\n                         hv_mask: np.ndarray,\n                         n_keep: int,\n                         batch_key: str = 'batch') -> np.ndarray:\n      \"\"\"\n      Batch-Robust Feature Selection (BRFS) over an HVG candidate set:\n      - For each batch b, compute per-gene variance on normalized+log1p counts.\n      - Define robust variability = min_b var_b(g) to prefer genes variable in all batches.\n      - Select top n_keep genes by robust variability (hard selection).\n      \"\"\"\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs[batch_key].astype('category').values)\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      # Work in dense for HVGs only (≈2000 genes)\n      Xcounts = (adata.layers['counts'] if 'counts' in adata.layers else adata.X)[:, hv_mask]\n      Xd = (Xcounts.A if sp.issparse(Xcounts) else Xcounts).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n\n      # Compute per-batch\
    \ variances after per-row normalize_total + log1p within batch\n      vars_list = []\n      for b in cats:\n          msk = (batches == b)\n          Xb = Xd[msk]\n          Xb_n = _normalize_total_log1p_dense(Xb)  # (nbatch, n_hv)\n          vb = Xb_n.var(axis=0, ddof=1).astype(np.float32)\n          vars_list.append(vb)\n      robust_var = np.minimum.reduce(vars_list)  # (n_hv,)\n\n      # Pick top n_keep by robust_var\n      n_hv = hv_mask.sum()\n      n_keep = int(min(n_keep, n_hv))\n      idx_sorted = np.argsort(-robust_var)  # descending\n      keep_local = np.zeros(n_hv, dtype=bool)\n      keep_local[idx_sorted[:n_keep]] = True\n\n      # Map back to genome-wide mask\n      brfs_mask = np.zeros(adata.n_vars, dtype=bool)\n      hv_idx = np.where(hv_mask)[0]\n      brfs_mask[hv_idx[keep_local]] = True\n      print(f\"[BRFS] hv_candidates={n_hv}, n_keep={n_keep}\")\n      return brfs_mask\n\n  def eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca_brfs(adata: ad.AnnData,\n                                \
    \    alpha_emb: float = 0.5,\n                                    n_pcs_emb: int = 60,\n                                    emb_n_neighbors: int = 15,\n                                    alpha_g: float = 0.5,\n                                    n_pcs_graph: int = 50,\n                                    l2: float = 1e-3,\n                                    gamma_max: float = 0.74,\n                                    lam_override: float = 0.605,\n                                    k_total: int = 50,\n                                    kdensity: int = 22,\n                                    delta: float = 0.10,\n                                    metric: str = 'cosine',\n                                    cap: float = 2.0,\n                                    rng_seed: int = 0,\n                                    keep_frac: float = 0.75,\n                                    batch_key: str = 'batch') -> ad.AnnData:\n      assert batch_key in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name'\
    \ in adata.var.columns\n\n      # Normalize + get HVG candidate set (batch-aware)\n      normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      if 'batch_hvg' in adata.var.columns:\n          hv_candidates = adata.var['batch_hvg'].to_numpy().astype(bool)\n      else:\n          sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key=batch_key, n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n          hv_candidates = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy().astype(bool)\n\n      # Hard BRFS selection (subset of HVGs)\n      n_hv = int(hv_candidates.sum())\n      n_keep = max(200, int(n_hv * float(keep_frac)))\n      brfs_mask = _compute_brfs_mask(adata, hv_mask=hv_candidates, n_keep=n_keep, batch_key=batch_key)\n\n      # Extract dense HVGs after BRFS\n      Xsel = (adata.layers['counts'] if 'counts' in adata.layers else adata.X)[:, brfs_mask]\n      Xd = (Xsel.A if sp.issparse(Xsel) else Xsel).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n\n      # Embedding (QC) + neighbors: feature-weight by var, PCA, per-batch centering,\
    \ neighbors\n      Xw_emb = _fw_weight_by_var(Xd, alpha=alpha_emb)\n      ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw_emb, obs=adata.obs[[batch_key]].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      Zemb = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca'].copy().astype(np.float32)\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs[batch_key].astype('category').values)\n      for b in np.unique(batches):\n          msk = (batches == b)\n          Zemb[msk] = Zemb[msk] - Zemb[msk].mean(axis=0, keepdims=True)\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Zemb\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=emb_n_neighbors, metric=metric)\n\n      # Graph path: GraphFWPCA -> PCA -> BRBG (fixed λ) -> DAQB (binary + true distances)\n      Xw_graph = _fw_weight_by_var(Xd, alpha=alpha_g)\n      Zg = pca_array(Xw_graph, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n\n      B = one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0,\
    \ ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n\n      lam = float(lam_override)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2).astype(np.float32)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n\n      try:\n          Cg, Dg = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches,\n                                               k_total=k_total, metric=metric,\n                                               delta=delta, k_density=kdensity,\n                                               cap=cap, rng_seed=rng_seed)\n      except TypeError:\n          Cg, Dg = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches,\n                                               k_total=k_total, metric=metric,\n                                               delta=delta, k_density=kdensity,\n                                               rng_seed=rng_seed)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      adata.uns['mpb_graphfwpca_brfs']\
    \ = dict(alpha_g=float(alpha_g),\n                                              lam_override=float(lam),\n                                              kd=int(kdensity), delta=float(delta),\n                                              cap=float(cap),\n                                              k_total=int(k_total),\n                                              rng_seed=int(rng_seed),\n                                              keep_frac=float(keep_frac),\n                                              n_vars_kept=int(brfs_mask.sum()))\n      return adata\n```\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_brfs_p075.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from mpb_graphfwpca_brfs import eliminate_mpb_graphfwpca_brfs as _run\n\n  # Ridge + BRFS (keep_frac=0.75) synergy test — seed=0\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      print(\"[ridge+BRFS]\
    \ lam=0.605, delta=0.10, kd=22, k=50, alpha_g=0.5, keep_frac=0.75, seed=0\")\n      return _run(adata,\n                  alpha_g=0.50,\n                  lam_override=0.605,\n                  delta=0.10,\n                  kdensity=22,\n                  k_total=50,\n                  metric='cosine',\n                  cap=2.0,\n                  rng_seed=0,\n                  keep_frac=0.75)\n```\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ridge + Batch‑Robust Feature Selection (keep 75% robust HVGs) — synergy test\"\ntags: \"ridge, brfs, feature-selection, graph, robustness\"\nabstract: \"Implements batch‑robust feature selection by selecting the top 75% HVGs robust across batches (maximizing min per‑batch variance after normalize_total+log1p), then runs the frozen ridge pipeline. Tests reviewer’s suggestion of hard feature selection synergizing with FWPCA.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_brfs_p075\
    \ import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as _run\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      return _run(adata)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning feature selection experiment**\n\nI’m thinking about how to respond appropriately to the user. They mentioned that their revised archive paper was accepted and are suggesting future work on batch-robust feature selection combined with SOTA ridge FWPCA. I should consider how to plan and possibly implement an experiment by applying batch-robust feature selection (BRFS) to the SOTA pipeline. It seems I need to check existing code and review Axiom II's new Archive #37 for method details before moving forward.**Implementing batch-robust feature selection**\n\nIn the Archive Room, I see I can read Archive 37 for crucial details. I plan to implement a module named mpb_graphfwpca_brfs.py that includes a batch-robust feature selection (BRFS) step before PCA. Importantly, I need to create my own implementation without copying code from others. This could involve calculating gene variance per batch and selecting highly variable features across them. I'll explore methods\
  \ like using rank aggregation to identify features with stable high variance across all batches.**Proposing batch-robust HVG selection**\n\nI’m thinking about a new approach for selecting batch-robust highly variable genes (HVGs). The idea is to compute HVG rankings for each gene per batch, using something like Seurat v3, and then combine these through methods like \"min-rank\" or \"median rank\" to choose the top N genes. This way, we reduce the influence of any single batch. I'll implement a function to calculate per-batch gene variances post-log1p normalization and rely on Scanpy's features while creating a more robust aggregator using methods like Borda count. After establishing the selected gene set, I'll run the standard pipeline and compare the results to the baseline ridge model.**Planning minimal synergy test**\n\nI'm thinking to implement a runner called best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_brfs_minrank.py for a minimal synergy test. We could also test synergy with kd=18 and delta 0.08, as\
  \ Daedalus suggests, but my main focus is on guardrails. I plan to read Archive #37 to clarify concepts, so I'll call /execute_action{read 37}. Later, we can update our published capsule #38 to reflect future directions after the experiments. For implementation, I will replicate mpb_graphfwpca_override, modifying HVG selection to ensure robustness across batches using Scanpy to compute variances for gene ranks.**Planning batch-robust feature selection**\n\nI’m realizing that adata.var['batch_hvg'] exists, which may be useful for our robust selection method. To simplify my approach, I could compute log1p normalized counts and variances for each gene per batch, then identify the top 2000 genes by robust variance. I'll implement a function to handle this, using Scanpy for normalization. \n\nAdditionally, it looks like we need to refine our selection based on the batch-aware highly variable genes to ensure we're using only relevant genes in GraphFWPCA. I’ll implement a parameter to keep a\
  \ fraction of the top robust features, adjusting the model accordingly. The next steps involve executing actions to read Archive #37 and writing the necessary scripts.**Implementing variance computations and normalization**\n\nI’m considering calculating variance along axis 0 for arrays extracted from counts for each batch, and I'll convert data to float32 to ensure memory safety. I'll compute per-batch variances, keeping an eye on memory management. \n\nNext, I'll implement a function to normalize the data using log1p. For this, I'll create a simple function that scales rows before applying the log transformation. Once that's done, I’ll create runners for the scripts and submit them, updating capsule #38 with a note on future BRFS synergy experiments, too. Let's make sure to include logging as part of this."
