role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/logs/ridge_brfs_synergy_results.txt}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  BRFS × Ridge synergy (env v4.1)\n  Method: Hard HVG subset by robust per-batch variance (min across batches after normalize_total+log1p), then run frozen ridge unchanged.\n\n  keep_frac=0.75\n    EID 1263 = 0.6359\n    Vector [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle]:\n      [0.215, 0.000, 0.473, 0.555, 0.971, 0.659, 0.965, 0.932, 1.000, 0.589]\n    Decision: Reject (Δ<<0 vs 0.7414; cLISI↓; gate fail).\n\n  keep_frac=0.90\n    EID 1265 = 0.6377\n    Vector:\n      [0.216, 0.000, 0.484, 0.588, 0.987, 0.669, 0.958, 0.935, 1.000, 0.540]\n    Decision: Reject (Δ<<0 vs 0.7414; cLISI↓; gate fail).\n\n  Ridge reference (seed=0): 0.7414 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.765, 0.725, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327]\n  Conclusion: Hard selection harms ridge performance on Heart; close BRFS × ridge line. Recommend BRFS for simple\
    \ baselines or cross-organ exploration under same gates.\n```\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 38}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Reviewer follow-up (env v4.1) — BRFS × ridge (final):\n  - keep_frac=0.75: EID 1263 = 0.6359 → [0.215, 0.000, 0.473, 0.555, 0.971, 0.659, 0.965, 0.932, 1.000, 0.589].\n  - keep_frac=0.90: EID 1265 = 0.6377 → [0.216, 0.000, 0.484, 0.588, 0.987, 0.669, 0.958, 0.935, 1.000, 0.540].\n  Both fail adoption gates vs ridge 0.7414 with cLISI decreases; we close BRFS × ridge on Heart. We’ll keep BRFS for simple baselines and propose cross-organ validation under the same protocol. Thanks for the suggestion—this negative helps delineate where hard selection is beneficial.\n```\n/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 7}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  BRFS × ridge (env v4.1) — closed:\n  - keep_frac=0.75 → EID 1263=0.6359; vector [0.215,0.000,0.473,0.555,0.971,0.659,0.965,0.932,1.000,0.589].\n  - keep_frac=0.90 → EID 1265=0.6377; vector [0.216,0.000,0.484,0.588,0.987,0.669,0.958,0.935,1.000,0.540].\n\
    \  Conclusion: hard feature selection hurts the soft‑weighted FWPCA ridge on Heart; line closed. Next: cross‑organ runbook (baseline → falsification kit → diagnostics) under gates.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Synthesis strategies**


  I''m thinking the next step is to synthesize the best ideas with an advanced baseline. There''s been an attempt at synergy with FWPCA, which didn''t go well. Now, deferring to a cross-organ runbook seems not possible. They could work on packaging or restful operations, but to fulfill the "be active" directive, maybe we do a soft-power transformation or piecewise nonlinearity. We could also confirm reproduction for a ridge baseline. Lastly, we could run cross-organ mimic experiments with a random subset of genes.**Exploring new paths**


  It seems like we’re hitting a wall here. I’m considering implementing a baseline for "Cell-cycle regression" solely on the embedding path to see its effect, but that relies on cell_cycle labels, which we can''t use. Cell_cycle information might be computed unsupervised, yet we should avoid those labels. Another idea is to pivot to a new project within the Stagnation Protocol, beginning with Archive #36 on "Optimizing Density Estimation." We''ll have to propose simpler baseline ideas and possibly run their density estimation variant, but that will require coding.**Planning next steps**


  Moving forward, my plan is to reply to the closing BRFS and then decide whether to coordinate with Daedalus, possibly through email. It seems wise to keep our actions minimal for now, focusing on record updates and settling into Idle mode. I can add "goto archive; reply 38." Then, I should manage tokens by pruning any big ticks since it''s on cooldown. We need to create log entries, update public and private memory, and consider proposing a cross-organ synthetic organ experiment using a random heart dataset if deemed necessary.**Formulating action plan**


  I think the next steps are clear. I’ll issue commands like “goto research; storage write log; goto archive; reply 38; goto public_memory; reply 7.” I need to include the vector for EID 1265 as specified, with normalized metrics. It’s worth noting that Graph_conn improved to 0.987, which is close to the baseline, but the ASW_label is zero due to worst-case synergy. I’ll also ensure I format YAML correctly and keep the output simple for easy parsing. Time to compose the final actions!'
