  \ \"ComBat + kNN\" with PBVE-lite or considering \"cell-wise library size matched normalization\" for another approach. I'm pondering what our next steps should be!**Proposing New Normalization Ideas**\n\nI'm considering \"Quantile normalization across batches per gene\" which feels similar to ComBat. Another option is creating a \"Batch-blind soft-threshold on PCs by robust per-axis kurtosis gating\" to adjust distances. According to our Stagnation Protocol, we need to propose three new ideas, and we have some in mind already. I want to implement \"PBVE-lite with t=0.25\" or \"per-PC winsorization.\" I think we should test the improved PBVE-lite with specific thresholds to see if we can achieve better results beyond our earlier baseline of 0.6098.**Implementing New Normalization Strategies**\n\nI'm considering implementing \"idea_pbve_knn_thresholded.py,\" which would equalize only the PCs where the absolute value of log(σ_b/σ_global) exceeds tau. I'll try parameters tau=0.2, t=0.5, and\
  \ clamp ratios between [0.8, 1.25]. I might also explore combining this with mutual-k later, but it’s crucial to focus on one idea first. Additionally, I want to propose \"per-cell vector L2 normalization\" before kNN after PCA to standardize energy, which could improve distance measures. I also have ideas like \"subspace dropout\" or \"PC clipping per row at ±3 SD\" to tackle batch effects—thinking carefully not to overwhelm the process with too many ideas!**Planning Experiments and Implementations**\n\nI’m thinking we might not need to re-run the baseline anchor. Instead, I’ll focus on submitting the thresholded PBVE-lite and testing the PCA baseline by dropping the top three PCs. Additionally, I want to consider a robust distance z-scores variant for idea two, while maintaining minimal repetition in experiments. \n\nI’m also planning to implement \"idea_pbve_knn_thresh.py\" and \"idea_pca_drop3_knn.py\" and ensure code runs smoothly within our environment. We might prune old tokens\
  \ if we approach 75%. Let’s summarize these actions and keep thorough logs!"
