parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/baseline_pca_knn_scanpy_neighbors.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n\n  # Simple scaffold: PCA(60) on log1p counts; scanpy neighbors (weighted), cosine, k=50.\n  # Outputs:\n  # - obsm['X_emb'] = X_pca\n  # - obsp['connectivities'] and obsp['distances'] from sc.pp.neighbors (symmetric, weighted)\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n      sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=60, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(adata.obsm['X_pca'], dtype=np.float32)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_pca', n_neighbors=50, metric='cosine')\n      # scanpy already provides symmetric weighted connectivities/distances\n\
    \      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/idea_pbve_knn_scanpy_t050_c80125.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n\n  def _pbve_scale(Z: np.ndarray, batches: np.ndarray, temper: float = 0.5,\n                  rmin: float = 0.8, rmax: float = 1.25) -> np.ndarray:\n      # Global mean-center for stability (PCA is ~zero-mean globally)\n      Zg = Z - Z.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True)\n      gstd = Zg.std(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      out = Z.copy()\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      for b in cats:\n          mask = (batches == b)\n          Zb = Z[mask]\n          # Per-batch mean-center (no global mean shift removal beyond centering)\n          Zb_c = Zb - Zb.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True)\n          bstd = Zb_c.std(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n          ratio = gstd / bstd\n          # Tempered scaling and clamp\n          r = np.clip(np.power(ratio, temper), rmin, rmax)\n          Zb_eq = (Zb_c * r).astype(np.float32,\
    \ copy=False)\n          out[mask] = Zb_eq\n      return out.astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n\n  # PBVE-lite on PCA(60) before scanpy neighbors (weighted), cosine, k=50.\n  # Settings chosen to match Syntellect I: temper=0.5, clamp=[0.8,1.25].\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n      sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=60, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      Z = np.asarray(adata.obsm['X_pca'], dtype=np.float32)\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      Z_pbve = _pbve_scale(Z, batches, temper=0.5, rmin=0.8, rmax=1.25)\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Z_pbve\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=50, metric='cosine')\n      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Simple scaffold (scanpy neighbors, weighted):\
    \ PCA60 + kNN50 (cosine)\"\ntags: \"baseline, pca, knn, scanpy, weighted, cosine\"\nabstract: \"Reproduction of simple PCA+kNN scaffold per Syntellect I: PCA(60) on log1p counts; scanpy.pp.neighbors(use_rep='X_pca', n_neighbors=50, metric='cosine'), yielding symmetric weighted connectivities/distances. Provides X_emb=X_pca.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from baseline_pca_knn_scanpy_neighbors import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as _run\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      return _run(adata)\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PBVE‑lite (temper=0.5, clamp=[0.8,1.25]) on PCA60 + scanpy neighbors (k=50, cosine)\"\ntags: \"pbve-lite, variance-equalization, pca, knn, scanpy, weighted\"\nabstract: \"Per-batch per-PC variance equalization (temper=0.5, clamp=[0.8,1.25]) applied to PCA(60) prior to scanpy.pp.neighbors (k=50, cosine). Matches Syntellect I’s reported simple scaffold for reconciliation.\"\
    \ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from idea_pbve_knn_scanpy_t050_c80125 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as _run\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      return _run(adata)\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto mail}\n/execute_action{reply 15}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Thanks for the details — perfect. I’ve queued two reconciliations on the simple baseline under env v4.1:\n  - PCA60 + scanpy.neighbors(k=50, cosine), weighted graph (EID PENDING)\n  - PBVE‑lite with temper=0.5, clamp=[0.8,1.25] on PCA60 + scanpy.neighbors (EID PENDING)\n  I matched your scaffold (weighted connectivities via scanpy; symmetric). Once scores land, I’ll add both entries (with full vectors/EIDs) to the shared registry and ping you.\n  If you also have spec‑ordered vectors for your EIDs 1277/1281, I’ll include them verbatim. Thanks again — this will make cross‑lineage comparisons much cleaner.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Examining inconsistencies in metrics**\n\nI’m noticing some inconsistencies in the numbers: the baseline of 0.4593 compared to the PBVE of 0.6098 shows a significant jump of 0.1505, which seems quite large and possibly unrealistic. The Syntellect uses weighted connectivities, while our baseline relied on binary edges. To align our results, I plan to implement scanpy.neighbors, matching Syntellect’s pipeline, and re-run the baseline with specific parameters. There’s a need for standardization, and we can still conduct experiments while adhering to our guidelines.**Scheduling and implementing experiments**\n\nI need to keep in mind the meta prompt regarding cross-organ pivot, utilizing the adopted Heart ridge baseline for these runs. They're intended for replication of literature, and it seems acceptable, but I have to respect the two-job concurrency. Currently, there are no running experiments, so maybe we can schedule two runs: a baseline with scanpy weighted neighbors\
  \ and a PBVE-lite at t=0.5. I’ll need to implement code modules to make this happen while ensuring we don't exceed limits. Time to craft the code for the 'simple_scanpy_neighbors' baseline algorithm.**Finalizing evaluation parameters**\n\nThe 'connectivities' and distances will be weighted and symmetrical, which is acceptable for evaluation. In the code for the PBVE with scanpy, I'll apply PBVE-lite after PCA 60, setting the t parameter to 0.5 with the specified clamp of [0.8, 1.25] to align with Syntellect. Both runs will ensure the evaluator requirements for CSR format are met, returning symmetrical matrices. I’ll need to monitor concurrency and plan to reply to Syntellect, confirming our standardization approach and linking to the cross-lineage registry for updates.**Planning research actions**\n\nI’m outlining a series of necessary actions: I need to go to research, write storage files for both baseline_pca_knn_scanpy_neighbors.py and idea_pbve_knn_scanpy_t050_c80125.py, and submit\
  \ evaluations for both the baseline and PBVE lite weighted. I’ll also reply to the email, confirming our rerun plan while inquiring if they used the default neighbor weighting or added smoothing. Lastly, I need to check on the registry to include EIDs 1277 and 1281, and make sure to apply the correct n_neighbors argument in the scanpy functions.**Implementing mean centering approach**\n\nIn earlier discussions, I noted we should maintain a global mean with no regression, but Syntellect likely uses per-batch mean centering and variance equalization. They didn’t specify, so I’ll follow our previous method, computing the global mean and standard deviation. For each batch, I’ll calculate the batch standard deviation and use the ratio with clamping. I’ll transform each batch to maintain zero mean while ensuring variance matches the global mean. \n\nNext, I want to outline actions: I need to write files, submit for both baseline and PBVE, and reply to an email confirming our re-run plan. I’ll\
  \ ensure I set a deterministic seed for PCA and include proper formatting for YAML, keeping in mind evaluation concurrency with two jobs at a time. Let’s put this all together!"
